enum PIAStudyBank {
    static let sections: [StudySection] = [
        // Evaluate the Current System Landscape (13 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "system_landscape",
            title: "Evaluate the Current System Landscape",
            color: Color(red: 0.20, green: 0.47, blue: 0.96),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.92, green: 0.95, blue: 1.00),
            icon: "map.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "pia_obj_1",
                    title: "Given a set of business requirements, identify the current system landscape and determine what standards, limitations, boundaries, and protocols exist",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "1.1", number: 1, question: "Identifying integration standards (REST vs SOAP vs event-driven) and protocol constraints when analyzing an existing multi-system landscape", answer: "REST uses lightweight JSON over HTTP and is the modern default. SOAP uses XML with a strict WSDL contract, common in legacy enterprise systems. Event-driven (Platform Events, Kafka) decouples producers from consumers asynchronously. When analyzing a landscape, document each system's supported protocols, payload formats, and versioning policies. Constraints emerge when a legacy system only speaks SOAP or when firewalls block non-standard ports."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "1.2", number: 2, question: "Recognizing legacy system protocols (SOAP/XML, EDI, flat file) and determining interoperability requirements when modernizing integrations", answer: "Legacy systems often expose SOAP/XML web services, EDI (X12/EDIFACT) for B2B transactions, or flat files (CSV, fixed-width) via SFTP. Interoperability requires a transformation layer (MuleSoft DataWeave, XSLT) to convert these formats into Salesforce-compatible JSON/REST. When modernizing, map each legacy protocol to a Salesforce API equivalent and identify where a middleware layer is needed to bridge the gap."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "1.3", number: 3, question: "Mapping an existing system landscape to identify the canonical data model used across systems and where data format mismatches require transformation", answer: "A canonical data model (CDM) is a neutral shared format used by middleware so each system only maps to/from the CDM instead of every other system. Identify the CDM by looking for a shared data dictionary or integration hub. Format mismatches (e.g., CRM Contact vs ERP Customer) require transformation logic. In MuleSoft, DataWeave handles this. Without a CDM, point-to-point integrations create N-squared mapping complexity."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "1.4", number: 4, question: "Differentiating between synchronous and asynchronous integration boundaries and when each is mandated by existing system constraints", answer: "Synchronous integrations wait for a response before continuing (REST callout, SOAP). They are mandated when the calling process needs an immediate result (e.g., credit check before order completion). Asynchronous integrations fire and move on (Platform Events, Bulk API, outbound messaging). They are required when the target is slow/unreliable or the operation is long-running. Salesforce mandates async when a callout would exceed 120 seconds or when DML precedes the callout in the same transaction."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "1.5", number: 5, question: "Identifying firewall, network boundary, and DMZ constraints that affect inbound vs outbound integration choices", answer: "Inbound integrations (external system calling Salesforce) typically only need Salesforce IP ranges allowlisted at the external firewall. Outbound integrations (Salesforce calling external systems) require Remote Site Settings and may be blocked if the target is behind a private firewall. DMZ-hosted systems may need a reverse proxy or API Gateway. On-premise targets may require a VPN tunnel, Salesforce Private Connect, or a hybrid MuleSoft runtime deployed inside the network perimeter."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "1.6", number: 6, question: "Determining which Salesforce platform limits (API call limits, governor limits, concurrent request limits) constrain an integration design given a system landscape", answer: "Key limits: daily REST/SOAP API calls (based on edition/license count), max 5 concurrent long-running Apex requests, 120-second callout timeout, 100 callouts per transaction, 12MB heap size (async), and 50,000 Platform Events published per hour. When multiple systems all call Salesforce APIs they share the org's daily limit. Use the /limits REST endpoint to monitor consumption. High-volume scenarios require Bulk API or event-driven patterns to stay within thresholds."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "1.7", number: 7, question: "Identifying Salesforce org topology constraints (multi-org, sandbox, production, Government Cloud) that affect integration architecture decisions", answer: "Multi-org topologies require routing logic and separate Connected Apps/credentials per org. Sandbox orgs have separate API endpoints and credentials; integrations must be reconfigured per environment (use Named Credentials and Custom Metadata for portability). Government Cloud (FedRAMP) has stricter IP ranges, different login URLs, and may restrict certain features. Each topology affects OAuth redirect URIs, IP allowlists, and endpoint URLs.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "pia_obj_2",
                    title: "Given an existing system landscape, analyze for constraints and/or pain-points to satisfy a business requirement",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "2.1", number: 1, question: "Analyzing tight coupling pain points in point-to-point integrations and recommending architectural improvements using middleware or an ESB", answer: "Point-to-point integrations create a direct connection web where each system knows every other. A change to one system breaks all its connections. The fix is a hub-and-spoke model using an ESB or iPaaS (MuleSoft). Each system connects only to the middleware hub, reducing connections from N-squared to 2N and enabling centralized transformation, routing, and monitoring."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "2.2", number: 2, question: "Identifying data duplication and synchronization conflicts as integration pain points when multiple systems hold the same data (system of record ambiguity)", answer: "When multiple systems store the same data (e.g., customer records in Salesforce and ERP), conflicts arise when both are updated simultaneously. This is system of record (SoR) ambiguity. The fix is to designate one authoritative source per data domain (e.g., Salesforce owns contacts, ERP owns orders) and have all other systems sync from that SoR. Conflict resolution strategies (last-write-wins, timestamp-based merge) must also be defined."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "2.3", number: 3, question: "Identifying data latency and throughput bottlenecks in an existing integration landscape that prevent real-time synchronization requirements from being met", answer: "Latency bottlenecks occur when polling intervals are too long or batch jobs run infrequently. Throughput bottlenecks occur when a system cannot process incoming messages fast enough. Solutions: switch from polling to event-driven (CDC, Platform Events), reduce batch window sizes, use Bulk API for high-volume loads, or add a message queue to absorb traffic spikes."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "2.4", number: 4, question: "Recognizing Salesforce API limit consumption as a constraint when multiple existing integrations share the same org's daily API allocation", answer: "Salesforce daily API limits are shared across all consumers of an org. If 10 systems each poll Salesforce every minute they quickly exhaust the daily allocation. Solutions: consolidate polling into a single middleware consumer, switch high-frequency polls to streaming (CDC, Platform Events), use Bulk API for mass operations (cheaper per record), and monitor usage per Connected App."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "2.5", number: 5, question: "Assessing on-premise vs cloud system hosting as a landscape constraint that determines whether a Salesforce-hosted integration (callouts) or hybrid middleware (MuleSoft on-prem, Mule Gateway) is required", answer: "Salesforce can make outbound HTTPS callouts to publicly reachable endpoints. If the target is on-premise behind a corporate firewall, a direct callout fails. Options: deploy a MuleSoft Runtime Engine on-premise to bridge the gap, use Salesforce Private Connect (AWS PrivateLink), or set up a VPN/reverse proxy. Cloud-hosted systems are simpler since they have public HTTPS endpoints Salesforce can call directly via Remote Site Settings."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "2.6", number: 6, question: "Identifying the absence of event-driven capability in existing point-to-point integrations as a scalability pain point and assessing the migration path to pub/sub architecture", answer: "Polling-based integrations repeatedly query for changes, wasting API calls when there are no updates and introducing latency. The migration path to pub/sub: introduce Platform Events or CDC so Salesforce publishes changes automatically. External systems subscribe via CometD or MuleSoft connector. For non-Salesforce systems, introduce a message broker (RabbitMQ, Kafka, Anypoint MQ) as the event bus to decouple producers from consumers.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),
        // Authentication, Authorization & Requirements (16 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "auth_requirements",
            title: "Authentication, Authorization & Requirements",
            color: Color(red: 0.55, green: 0.27, blue: 0.68),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.95, green: 0.92, blue: 1.00),
            icon: "lock.shield.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "pia_obj_3",
                    title: "Given a set of requirements, evaluate the authentication and authorization needs based on the system landscape",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "3.1", number: 1, question: "Choosing between user-level OAuth (user context) and system-level OAuth (service account / client credentials) based on integration data access requirements", answer: "User-level OAuth runs in the context of a specific user, respecting their sharing rules, FLS, and record access. Use it when the integration must reflect user-specific data visibility. System-level OAuth uses a service account with a fixed permission set, bypassing individual user context. Use it for automated server-to-server processes. System-level is simpler to maintain but requires careful permission scoping to avoid over-privileged access."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "3.2", number: 2, question: "Selecting OAuth 2.0 Web Server flow when a user-context integration requires browser redirect for third-party app access", answer: "The Web Server flow (authorization code grant) redirects the user's browser to Salesforce's authorization endpoint where they log in and consent. Salesforce returns an authorization code to the app's redirect URI. The app exchanges it for an access token server-side. Use this when a web app needs to act on behalf of a Salesforce user. It is the most secure user-facing OAuth flow because tokens are never exposed in the browser URL."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "3.3", number: 3, question: "Selecting OAuth 2.0 JWT Bearer flow for server-to-server integrations where no user interaction is possible", answer: "The JWT Bearer flow allows a server to obtain an access token by presenting a signed JWT assertion, eliminating the need for user interaction or a refresh token exchange. The server signs the JWT with its private key; Salesforce validates it using the registered public certificate. Ideal for scheduled jobs, daemon processes, and CI/CD pipelines. Pre-authorize the integration user in the Connected App to avoid consent prompts."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "3.4", number: 4, question: "Selecting OAuth 2.0 Client Credentials flow for system-level integrations that use a service account", answer: "The Client Credentials flow (available from Winter '23) allows a server to exchange a client ID and secret for an access token with no user context. Unlike JWT Bearer, no certificate is required. The token runs as the Connected App's run-as user. Use it for machine-to-machine integrations where simplicity is preferred and certificate management is a burden. Ensure the run-as user has only the permissions needed for the integration."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "3.5", number: 5, question: "Determining OAuth scopes for a Connected App based on integration data access needs and principle of least privilege", answer: "Common scopes: api (full REST API access), refresh_token (obtain refresh tokens), chatter_api (Chatter only), full (all access including admin), openid/profile/email (identity). Apply least privilege: only grant the scopes the integration actually uses. Avoid granting 'full' or 'api' when a narrower scope suffices. Restrict scopes in Setup > Connected Apps > Manage > Edit Policies."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "3.6", number: 6, question: "Determining OAuth policies (IP relaxation, admin-approved users, refresh token) for Connected App security in inbound integrations", answer: "IP relaxation: 'Enforce IP restrictions' respects Login IP Ranges; 'Relax with second factor' allows broader access with MFA; 'Relax IP restrictions' removes network enforcement (use cautiously). Admin-approved users only: restricts access to profiles/permission sets explicitly granted. Refresh token policy: sets expiry and reuse rules. Always prefer the most restrictive settings that still meet operational needs."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "3.7", number: 7, question: "Evaluating when to use Named Credentials vs hardcoded credentials vs externally managed secrets for outbound callout authentication", answer: "Named Credentials store endpoint URLs and authentication details securely in Salesforce metadata, eliminating credentials from Apex code. They support OAuth, Basic Auth, and custom headers, and handle token refresh automatically. Hardcoded credentials in Apex are a security risk and fail across environments. Externally managed secrets (HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager) suit complex multi-system credential rotation but require custom retrieval logic. Named Credentials are the Salesforce-native best practice."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "3.8", number: 8, question: "Configuring IP restrictions for inbound integration access to enforce network-level security requirements", answer: "Salesforce inbound API access can be restricted by IP at two levels: Login IP Ranges (on profiles/permission sets) restrict which IPs a user can log in from, and Connected App IP policies control relaxation of those ranges for OAuth. For inbound integrations, configure the integration user's profile with the external system's IP range. Pair with TLS to prevent man-in-the-middle attacks on the network path."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "3.9", number: 9, question: "Evaluating SAML-based SSO vs OAuth for authentication when integrating Salesforce with an enterprise identity provider (IdP)", answer: "SAML SSO is for human authentication: a user logs into their corporate IdP (Okta, Azure AD) and gets a SAML assertion that Salesforce trusts to create a session. It does not issue API tokens. OAuth is for API and application authentication, issuing access tokens for programmatic access. Use SAML when employees must SSO via their corporate directory. Use OAuth when an application needs to call Salesforce APIs. The two can coexist: SAML for humans, JWT Bearer OAuth for automation."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "3.10", number: 10, question: "Determining when certificate-based authentication (mTLS) is required by existing system security standards and how Salesforce stores and uses X.509 certificates for outbound connections", answer: "Mutual TLS (mTLS) requires both client and server to present X.509 certificates, providing stronger assurance than OAuth alone. Required when target systems enforce client certificate authentication (common in financial services, healthcare, government). In Salesforce, upload the certificate to Setup > Certificate and Key Management. Reference it in a Named Credential or use HttpRequest.setClientCertificateName() in Apex. Salesforce also publishes its own certificates so external systems can pin them for inbound validation.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "pia_obj_4",
                    title: "Given a use case, identify functional and non-functional requirements needed for integration",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "4.1", number: 1, question: "Distinguishing functional integration requirements (data transformation rules, business process triggers) from non-functional requirements (latency SLAs, availability, scalability)", answer: "Functional requirements define what the integration must do: which fields to map, what business event triggers the sync, how data is transformed. Non-functional requirements define how well it must do it: response time under 2 seconds, 99.9% uptime, ability to handle 10x volume spikes. Functional requirements drive the data model and pattern selection. Non-functional requirements drive architectural choices like sync vs async, middleware selection, and retry design."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "4.2", number: 2, question: "Translating business SLA requirements (e.g., data must sync within 15 minutes) into technical integration pattern selection criteria", answer: "A 15-minute SLA eliminates nightly batch but allows short-interval scheduled batch or event-driven patterns. A sub-second SLA mandates synchronous request-reply. A 4-hour SLA opens up hourly batch. Map SLAs to patterns: real-time = synchronous callout or streaming; near-real-time = Platform Events/CDC/short-interval polling; batch = Scheduled Apex, Bulk API, or ETL. Target 50-70% of the SLA window to allow for retries and processing overhead."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "4.3", number: 3, question: "Recognizing idempotency as a non-functional requirement and its implications for integration design (duplicate message handling)", answer: "Idempotency means processing the same message multiple times produces the same result as processing it once. Critical in integrations because network failures cause retries that can create duplicate records. Achieve idempotency in Salesforce by: using upsert with an external ID field, storing a message ID in a custom field and checking before processing, and using Platform Event ReplayId to detect already-processed events. Design all integration endpoints to be idempotent from the start."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "4.4", number: 4, question: "Identifying availability and disaster recovery requirements that dictate integration architecture choices (retry logic, failover, redundancy)", answer: "High availability (HA) requirements require retry logic with exponential backoff, dead-letter queues for unprocessable messages, and failover to alternate endpoints. DR requirements define RPO (how much data can be lost) and RTO (how fast to recover). For integrations: store failed messages in a queue or custom object, use Platform Event replay for streaming recovery, and design idempotent reprocessing. Multi-region middleware deployments address geographic failover."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "4.5", number: 5, question: "Identifying observability requirements (logging, tracing, alerting) as non-functional requirements that must be designed into the integration architecture from the start", answer: "Observability enables you to understand what an integration is doing without modifying it. Logging: capture request/response payloads, timestamps, and status in a custom object or external logging system. Tracing: use correlation IDs propagated across systems to trace a single transaction end-to-end. Alerting: set thresholds on error rates, latency, and queue depth with automated notifications. In Salesforce, use Platform Events or custom objects for logs. In MuleSoft, use Anypoint Monitoring. Design these from day one."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "4.6", number: 6, question: "Determining the maintainability and operational requirements (on-call runbooks, integration ownership, support SLAs) that influence build vs buy decisions for integration middleware", answer: "Maintainability requirements ask: can the team operate this over 3-5 years? Custom-built Apex integrations are maintainable if the team has Salesforce skills but lack built-in monitoring and routing. MuleSoft provides monitoring, error handling, and connectors out-of-the-box but requires MuleSoft expertise. Support SLAs favor iPaaS platforms with dedicated support contracts. Build (Apex) when control and cost are priorities; buy (iPaaS) when operational maturity, monitoring, and speed-to-market matter.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),
        // Integration Strategy & Business Alignment (13 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "integration_strategy",
            title: "Integration Strategy & Business Alignment",
            color: Color(red: 0.17, green: 0.63, blue: 0.45),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.90, green: 0.98, blue: 0.93),
            icon: "arrow.triangle.branch",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "pia_obj_5",
                    title: "Based on a given integration requirement, identify and classify data into Confidential/Secure/Public",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "5.1", number: 1, question: "Classifying Salesforce data (PII, financial records, public marketing data) and applying appropriate integration security controls based on data classification", answer: "Data classification tiers: Confidential (PII, SSNs, health data, financial account numbers) requires encryption in transit and at rest, strict access controls, audit logging, and data masking in non-production. Secure (internal business data, contracts) requires access controls and TLS. Public (marketing content, product catalogs) has minimal restrictions. Map each object/field to a classification tier and configure FLS, Shield Platform Encryption, and integration user permissions accordingly."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "5.2", number: 2, question: "Determining transport security requirements (TLS version, mutual TLS, encryption at rest) based on data classification in an integration context", answer: "Confidential data requires TLS 1.2+ (Salesforce enforces this), mutual TLS for high-assurance scenarios, and encryption at rest using Salesforce Shield or external key management. Secure data requires TLS 1.2+ minimum. Public data still needs TLS to prevent tampering. For encryption at rest: Shield Platform Encryption encrypts fields at the database level. Note: encrypted fields cannot be used in SOQL WHERE clause filter conditions and Bulk API exports return encrypted values based on the user's 'View Encrypted Data' permission."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "5.3", number: 3, question: "Identifying which data classification requires field-level masking, tokenization, or encryption before transmission to external systems", answer: "Confidential data (PII, financial data) often cannot be transmitted in plain text to external systems. Options: masking (replace part of the value, e.g., last 4 digits of SSN only), tokenization (replace the value with a non-sensitive token; a vault maps tokens back to values, preferred for PCI-DSS), encryption (transmit encrypted and let the recipient decrypt with a shared key). Tokenization is preferred for payment card data. Masking is appropriate for lower-risk display scenarios."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "5.4", number: 4, question: "Determining data residency requirements based on data classification and how they constrain the geographic deployment of integration middleware", answer: "Data residency laws (GDPR in EU, LGPD in Brazil) require certain data to remain within specified geographic boundaries. For integrations, middleware must be deployed in a compliant region, data cannot be routed through non-compliant regions, and logs containing sensitive data must also reside in the approved region. MuleSoft CloudHub 2.0 allows region selection per environment. Salesforce Hyperforce enables country-specific data residency. Violating residency requirements can result in significant regulatory penalties.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "pia_obj_6",
                    title: "Given a use case, identify key factors for CRM success that should be included as integration requirements",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "6.1", number: 1, question: "Identifying the need for a 360-degree customer view as a CRM success requirement and determining which external systems must be integrated to achieve it", answer: "A 360-degree customer view means sales reps see all relevant customer data in Salesforce without switching systems. Identify which external systems hold customer-relevant data: ERP (orders, invoices, account financials), marketing automation (email engagement, campaign history), support (case history), e-commerce (purchase history), and financial systems (payment status). Without this integration, CRM adoption suffers because reps find the tool incomplete."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "6.2", number: 2, question: "Defining the system of record for each data domain as a foundational CRM integration requirement to prevent data conflicts", answer: "A system of record (SoR) is the authoritative source of truth for a data domain. Without clear SoR definitions, multiple systems update the same data causing conflicts. Define: Salesforce is SoR for contacts and opportunities; ERP is SoR for billing data and orders. Integration flows must respect the SoR: updates originate from the SoR and are read-only replicas in other systems. Document this in an integration design document and enforce it in data mapping rules."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "6.3", number: 3, question: "Identifying data quality requirements (completeness, accuracy, deduplication) as integration requirements critical to CRM adoption and success", answer: "Poor data quality kills CRM adoption: reps won't trust a system with incomplete or duplicate records. Data quality requirements for integrations include: completeness (required fields populated before syncing), accuracy (values transformed correctly, e.g., currency codes aligned), and deduplication (match-and-merge logic to prevent duplicate accounts/contacts). In Salesforce, use Duplicate Rules and Matching Rules. Integration designs must include data validation and rejection logic to prevent bad data entering Salesforce."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "6.4", number: 4, question: "Determining user experience requirements that mandate real-time vs near-real-time integration, particularly for sales reps accessing live data in Salesforce", answer: "If a sales rep is on a call and needs current inventory levels, a batch sync that runs every 4 hours is unacceptable. Map user scenarios to latency requirements: live inventory checks require synchronous callouts or Salesforce Connect (External Objects via OData). Quote pricing updates require near-real-time (Platform Events or CDC with sub-1-minute lag). Background record enrichment can use batch. UX requirements drive the integration pattern; never let technical convenience dictate a pattern that breaks the user experience.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "pia_obj_7",
                    title: "Given a use case, identify the business growth and regulatory factors that can impact choice of integration solutions",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "7.1", number: 1, question: "Assessing data volume growth and new geographies as factors requiring scalable, multi-region integration solutions", answer: "An integration handling 10,000 records today may need to handle 1,000,000 in three years. Scalability requires: Bulk API instead of REST for high-volume loads, MuleSoft with multiple workers for throughput, and event-driven patterns that scale consumers independently. New geographies introduce data residency requirements and latency challenges (transatlantic API calls add hundreds of milliseconds). Design with horizontal scalability from the start: stateless integration logic, externalizable configuration, and cloud-hosted middleware that scales on demand."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "7.2", number: 2, question: "Evaluating data residency constraints from GDPR, CCPA, and HIPAA in integration design", answer: "GDPR (EU) restricts transfer of EU citizen personal data outside the EU without adequate safeguards (standard contractual clauses, adequacy decision). CCPA (California) grants consumers rights over personal data. HIPAA (US healthcare) mandates protections for PHI including audit trails, encryption, and Business Associate Agreements. For integrations: audit data flows involving personal data, ensure middleware is deployed in compliant regions, encrypt PHI in transit and at rest, and log all access for audit purposes."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "7.3", number: 3, question: "Evaluating data retention and cross-border transfer restrictions from regulations in integration design", answer: "Regulations mandate how long data must be kept (HIPAA: 6 years) and require deletion upon request (GDPR right to erasure). Cross-border transfer restrictions require transfer mechanism requirements (EU-US Data Privacy Framework, SCCs). For integrations: tag data by origin jurisdiction, implement retention policies that cascade to all integrated systems, and ensure deletion requests propagate to all downstream systems. Log all transfers with timestamps for audit purposes."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "7.4", number: 4, question: "Determining how industry-specific compliance mandates (financial services, healthcare) influence the choice between cloud-based and on-premise integration middleware", answer: "Financial services (PCI-DSS, SOX) and healthcare (HIPAA) often require on-premise or private-cloud middleware to maintain full control over data processing and audit trails. PCI-DSS prohibits storing cardholder data in non-certified environments. HIPAA requires BAAs with all vendors handling PHI. MuleSoft on-premise runtime satisfies requirements where cloud-hosted iPaaS cannot. Salesforce Government Cloud and Hyperforce address FedRAMP and EU data residency. Always validate integration platform certifications against the applicable compliance standard."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "7.5", number: 5, question: "Assessing M&A requirements where newly acquired systems must be rapidly integrated with Salesforce", answer: "M&A integrations are time-pressured and involve systems built on different standards. Key considerations: rapid assessment of acquired system APIs (REST, SOAP, flat file), identification of data overlap and conflict points (duplicate customer records), decision on whether to migrate acquired data into Salesforce or federate it via integration. Short-term: use middleware (MuleSoft) for rapid connection without system changes. Long-term: migrate and consolidate. Maintain separate org topologies until data governance and deduplication are resolved.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),
        // Design & Architecture (26 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "design_architecture",
            title: "Design & Architecture",
            color: Color(red: 0.90, green: 0.49, blue: 0.13),
            lightColor: Color(red: 1.00, green: 0.95, blue: 0.90),
            icon: "building.2.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "pia_obj_8",
                    title: "Given an existing system landscape diagram, create an inventory of the systems and integration patterns",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "8.1", number: 1, question: "Mapping data flows between systems in a landscape to identify which integration patterns are in use and where gaps or risks exist", answer: "A data flow map documents each integration: source system, target system, direction, frequency, protocol, and pattern. Identify risks: unlabeled point-to-point connections (hidden dependencies), systems with no defined SoR, integrations without error handling or monitoring. Gaps appear as manual data entry that should be automated or data visible in one system but not in another where business needs dictate it should be. This inventory is the foundation for every subsequent architecture decision."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "8.2", number: 2, question: "Identifying hub-and-spoke vs point-to-point integration topology from a landscape diagram and understanding the operational trade-offs of each", answer: "Point-to-point: each system connects directly to every other, creating N*(N-1)/2 connections. Simple to build initially but every change to one system can break multiple integrations. Monitoring is fragmented. Hub-and-spoke: all systems connect to a central middleware hub, reducing connections to 2N. Enables centralized monitoring, transformation, and routing. The hub is a single point of failure unless made HA. Hub-and-spoke (ESB/iPaaS) is the enterprise standard for landscapes with more than 3-4 systems."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "8.3", number: 3, question: "Identifying request-reply and fire-and-forget integration patterns from a system landscape diagram", answer: "Request-reply: the caller sends a request and waits for a response (synchronous). Represented in diagrams as bidirectional arrows in a single interaction. Indicators: REST GET calls, SOAP service calls, Apex callouts with a return value. Fire-and-forget: the caller sends a message and does not wait for a response. Represented as one-way arrows. Indicators: outbound messages, Platform Event publishes, asynchronous Queueable jobs. Fire-and-forget improves caller performance but requires the receiving system to handle errors independently."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "8.4", number: 4, question: "Identifying batch and publish-subscribe integration patterns from a system landscape diagram", answer: "Batch: large sets of records transferred at scheduled intervals. Diagram indicators: scheduled job arrows, FTP/SFTP connections, Bulk API usage, data warehouse connections. Publish-subscribe (pub/sub): a producer publishes events to a topic without knowledge of subscribers; multiple subscribers consume independently. Diagram indicators: event bus (Kafka, Anypoint MQ), Platform Events, or CDC channels with multiple consuming systems. Pub/sub provides loose coupling; batch minimizes real-time infrastructure but introduces latency."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "8.5", number: 5, question: "Identifying event-driven patterns (Platform Events, Change Data Capture, Streaming API) in a landscape diagram", answer: "Platform Events: custom event objects published by Apex/Flow and consumed via CometD, Flow triggers, or Apex triggers. Change Data Capture (CDC): Salesforce automatically publishes change events for selected objects (Create, Update, Delete, Undelete) on the /data/ CometD channel. Streaming API: includes PushTopics (SOQL-based) and Generic Streaming for custom use cases. Diagram indicators: event bus channels, CometD subscriber arrows, event definitions in Salesforce setup. All three share at-least-once delivery with ReplayId for recovery."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "8.6", number: 6, question: "Distinguishing event-driven integration patterns from polling-based integration patterns in a landscape diagram", answer: "Polling: a consumer repeatedly queries the source system on a schedule (e.g., every 5 minutes: 'any new records?'). Indicated by recurring scheduled arrows in diagrams. Consumes API calls even when no data changed. Event-driven: the source system notifies consumers when something changes, eliminating unnecessary calls. Indicated by event bus or messaging channel connections. Event-driven is more efficient and lower-latency. Polling remains necessary when the source system cannot publish events; when both appear in a diagram, the polling integration is a candidate for modernization."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "8.7", number: 7, question: "Identifying process choreography integration patterns from a system landscape diagram", answer: "Process choreography: each system reacts to events without a central coordinator. System A publishes an event, System B reacts and publishes another, System C reacts to that, forming a chain. No single system knows the full process flow. Diagram indicators: chain of events across systems with no central orchestrator node. Benefits: loose coupling, independent scalability. Risks: difficult to debug, no single place to monitor the end-to-end process, and distributed failures are hard to recover from."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "8.8", number: 8, question: "Classifying Salesforce-to-Salesforce (SF2SF) integration patterns in a multi-org landscape and identifying when the native SF2SF feature or external middleware is more appropriate", answer: "Native SF2SF: uses Salesforce's built-in Connection and Subscription objects. Simple to configure, no middleware cost, but limited to standard objects, no complex transformation, and basic monitoring. External middleware (MuleSoft): handles custom objects, bidirectional sync, complex transformations, rich error handling, and full monitoring. Use native SF2SF for simple, low-maintenance, standard-object synchronization. Use middleware when data fidelity, complex mapping, or operational visibility is required.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "pia_obj_9",
                    title: "Given a use case and business process, evaluate system and process constraints",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "9.1", number: 1, question: "Evaluating Salesforce governor limits (concurrent API requests, callout timeout limits, heap size) as architectural constraints that force asynchronous integration patterns", answer: "Governor limits that force async patterns: 120-second callout timeout (long-running external processes must be async), 5 concurrent long-running Apex requests per org, 12MB heap size in async / 6MB sync (large payloads must be chunked), 100 SOQL queries per transaction. When synchronous patterns hit these limits, switch to Queueable Apex (allows callouts, chainable), Batch Apex, or Platform Events to decouple processing from the triggering transaction."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "9.2", number: 2, question: "Evaluating transaction boundary constraints in Salesforce (trigger execution context, two-phase commit limitations) that prevent synchronous cross-system transactions", answer: "Salesforce does not support distributed transactions (two-phase commit) across systems. A trigger can update Salesforce records and publish a Platform Event atomically, but it cannot rollback an external system call if the Salesforce DML fails afterward. Design for eventual consistency: use Platform Events published after commit to trigger external updates. If Salesforce DML fails, the event is not published. For outbound calls, use Queueable to decouple from the trigger's transaction."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "9.3", number: 3, question: "Evaluating Salesforce callout restrictions from synchronous Apex contexts (no callouts after DML in same transaction) as a constraint requiring an asynchronous workaround pattern", answer: "In Apex, you cannot make a callout after performing DML in the same transaction (throws 'You have uncommitted work pending' error). Workarounds: use @future(callout=true) for simple async callouts, Queueable Apex with callout=true for chainable async, or publish a Platform Event after DML (Platform Event subscribers run in a separate transaction and can make callouts). The Platform Event approach is most robust as it handles retries and decouples the integration cleanly."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "9.4", number: 4, question: "Assessing the 100-callout-per-transaction limit and other per-execution governor limits as constraints that shape the design of high-frequency integration triggers", answer: "Salesforce limits each transaction to 100 HTTP callouts. In bulk trigger scenarios processing 200 records, a one-callout-per-record design immediately fails. Solution: bulkify the integration by batching records into a single callout payload (REST collection endpoint, Composite API). For high-frequency trigger scenarios, use Platform Events to decouple and queue the integration, then process in batches using Scheduled Apex or Queueable chains."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "9.5", number: 5, question: "Assessing external system rate limits and throttling policies as constraints that require queuing, backoff, or bulk integration approaches", answer: "Many external systems enforce rate limits (e.g., 1,000 API calls/minute, 10 concurrent connections). When Salesforce triggers push high volumes of calls exceeding these limits, the external system returns HTTP 429. Solutions: implement exponential backoff retry logic in Apex, use a message queue (MuleSoft Anypoint MQ) to buffer requests and release them at the external system's acceptable rate, or switch to bulk endpoints that accept batches in one call. Document the external system's rate limits early and design to stay within them."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "9.6", number: 6, question: "Identifying process constraints such as approval workflows, business hours, and maintenance windows that affect integration scheduling and error handling design", answer: "Approval workflows can pause record updates: if an integration fires when a record enters an approval process, the record may be locked and updates will fail. Business hours constraints affect when integrations can run (e.g., a financial system only accepts updates during business hours). Maintenance windows temporarily interrupt integrations. Design with: record lock detection and retry, time-aware scheduling, and graceful degradation (queue messages during maintenance, replay after recovery).")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "pia_obj_10",
                    title: "Given a use case, identify integration security/authentication/authorization requirements",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "10.1", number: 1, question: "Determining the correct integration user setup (dedicated integration user, profile, permission set) to enforce least-privilege access for automated integrations", answer: "Every automated integration should run as a dedicated integration user (not a human admin account) with a profile or permission set granting only the objects and fields required. Grant object-level Create/Read/Update permissions only as needed. Add permission sets for specific API accesses. This limits blast radius if credentials are compromised and provides clear audit trails (all API calls attributed to the integration user). Never use the default System Administrator account for integrations."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "10.2", number: 2, question: "Specifying OAuth 2.0 scopes required for a Connected App based on the integration's data access needs and principle of least privilege", answer: "Apply least privilege: a data sync integration needs 'api' and 'refresh_token'. An integration that only reads needs 'api' with read-only permissions on the user's profile. Never grant 'full' unless truly necessary. Scopes are configured on the Connected App; restrict further using the integration user's profile permissions. Key scopes: api, web, refresh_token, chatter_api, custom_permissions, openid, profile, email."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "10.3", number: 3, question: "Determining IP allowlisting requirements for external systems calling Salesforce APIs to prevent unauthorized inbound access", answer: "IP allowlisting restricts which source IPs can authenticate to Salesforce via the Connected App. Configure Login IP Ranges on the integration user's profile to the external system's outbound IP range. Set the Connected App's IP policy to 'Enforce IP restrictions'. This ensures that even if credentials are stolen, they cannot be used from unauthorized network locations. Pair with OAuth (not basic auth) so credentials aren't transmitted in plain text."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "10.4", number: 4, question: "Translating data sensitivity requirements into Salesforce field-level security and sharing model constraints that integration user profiles must respect", answer: "The integration user's access must mirror data sensitivity controls. For confidential fields (SSN, salary, health data), use field-level security (FLS) on the integration user's profile to restrict visibility if the integration does not need them. Sharing model: if the integration user should only see records it owns or records shared to it, configure the org-wide default and sharing rules accordingly (don't use 'Modify All' unless truly required)."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "10.5", number: 5, question: "Identifying mutual TLS (mTLS) requirements for high-security integrations and how Salesforce supports or requires certificate-based authentication", answer: "mTLS requires both client (Salesforce) and server (external system) to present X.509 certificates, providing two-way authentication. Required by financial institutions, government agencies, and healthcare systems. For outbound callouts: upload a client certificate to Certificate and Key Management and reference it in the Named Credential or via HttpRequest.setClientCertificateName(). For inbound, use an API Gateway (MuleSoft) to enforce client certificate validation before forwarding to Salesforce."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "10.6", number: 6, question: "Identifying when SAML assertions must be passed through middleware to Salesforce for identity federation, and how the Salesforce identity provider and service provider roles are configured", answer: "In identity federation, an IdP (Okta, Azure AD) issues SAML assertions that a Service Provider (SP) trusts. Salesforce as SP: trusts assertions from the corporate IdP for SSO (configure in Setup > Single Sign-On Settings). Salesforce as IdP: issues assertions for users to access external apps (configure in Setup > Identity Provider). When middleware relays SAML assertions, it must correctly sign them with the expected issuer and audience values.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "pia_obj_11",
                    title: "Given a use case, identify performance needs (volumes, response times, latency) and propose appropriate integration solutions that will meet business requirements",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "11.1", number: 1, question: "Recommending Bulk API vs REST API vs Composite API based on data volume, response time requirements, and Salesforce API limits", answer: "REST API: best for low-to-medium volumes (<2,000 records), synchronous, immediate response. Composite API: combines up to 25 subrequests in one HTTP call, reducing API consumption for related record operations. Bulk API 2.0: designed for high-volume async processing (>2,000 records), job-based, CSV/JSON payload, separate limit treatment. Use REST for real-time individual operations, Composite for multi-object transactions, and Bulk for ETL and mass updates."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "11.2", number: 2, question: "Understanding the Salesforce Bulk API 2.0 job lifecycle and when it is the appropriate choice over Bulk API 1.0 or REST Collections", answer: "Bulk API 2.0 lifecycle: Create job (POST /jobs/ingest) > Upload CSV batches (PUT) > Close job (PATCH state=UploadComplete) > Poll for status (GET) > Retrieve success/failed results (GET). Simpler than v1.0 (no batch management). Use over v1.0 for simpler API management. Use REST Collections instead when you need JSON format, <200 records per request, and tighter per-record error handling. Bulk API 2.0 is appropriate when volumes exceed 2,000 records and async processing is acceptable."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "11.3", number: 3, question: "Choosing between synchronous callouts and asynchronous queueable/batch jobs for high-volume integration scenarios to avoid Salesforce callout limits", answer: "Synchronous callouts: immediate, but limited to 100 per transaction and 120-second timeout, and blocked after DML. Queueable Apex with callout=true: runs async after the triggering transaction commits, can chain, handles one callout context per execution. Batch Apex with callout=true: processes large record sets in chunks, each chunk gets a fresh callout limit. Always bulkify: one callout per batch/chunk, not per record."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "11.4", number: 4, question: "Determining when Platform Events or Change Data Capture (CDC) meets near-real-time latency requirements better than polling-based REST API calls", answer: "Polling introduces latency equal to the polling interval (a 5-minute poll means up to 5 minutes before changes propagate). Platform Events and CDC push changes immediately after the committing transaction, typically delivering within seconds. Use Platform Events when you define custom event types and control the publish logic. Use CDC when you need automated change tracking for standard and custom objects without code (just enable the entity in Setup). Both provide ReplayId for recovery."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "11.5", number: 5, question: "Evaluating data virtualization (Salesforce Connect / External Objects) as an alternative to full data replication when low latency and read-only access to external data is required", answer: "Salesforce Connect uses OData adapters to surface external system data as External Objects inside Salesforce without copying data. Each query translates to a real-time call to the external system. Benefits: always current data, no sync lag, no storage consumed. Limitations: read-mostly, subject to external system availability and latency, SOQL relationship traversal limited, and each External Object lookup counts against callout limits. Best for large read-only reference datasets impractical to replicate."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "11.6", number: 6, question: "Evaluating Heroku Connect as a data replication solution and when its eventual consistency model meets or conflicts with business latency requirements", answer: "Heroku Connect bidirectionally replicates data between Salesforce and a Heroku Postgres database. The Heroku app reads/writes Postgres; Heroku Connect syncs changes to/from Salesforce. Eventual consistency: changes are not instantaneous; polling intervals (configurable, minimum ~2 minutes) introduce lag. Suits high-read-volume applications (customer portals) where a few minutes of staleness is acceptable. Conflicts with requirements needing real-time accuracy (live inventory, financial totals).")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),
        // Integration Patterns & Solutions (25 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "integration_patterns",
            title: "Integration Patterns & Solutions",
            color: Color(red: 0.20, green: 0.60, blue: 0.86),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.90, green: 0.95, blue: 1.00),
            icon: "puzzlepiece.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "pia_obj_12",
                    title: "Given a use case, identify the integration pattern that meets business requirements",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "12.1", number: 1, question: "Selecting the request-reply integration pattern for synchronous real-time data retrieval scenarios and understanding its latency and coupling trade-offs", answer: "Request-reply: the caller sends a request and blocks until a response is received. Use when: the result is needed immediately before proceeding (credit check, inventory availability, address validation). Trade-offs: caller is tightly coupled to the called system's availability; latency of the called system directly impacts caller's performance. Mitigate with timeouts, circuit breakers, and fallback responses. In Salesforce: Apex HttpCallout for outbound, @RestResource for inbound."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "12.2", number: 2, question: "Applying the Remote Process Invocation (Request-Reply) pattern when a remote process result is required before proceeding", answer: "Remote Process Invocation (Request-Reply): Salesforce calls an external system to invoke a process (e.g., calculate shipping rates, validate tax ID) and waits for the result to continue. Implementation: Apex callout from a trigger, Flow, or @AuraEnabled method. The result is used immediately in the same transaction. Critical considerations: keep callouts out of trigger contexts (use Queueable or Continuation API for LWC async responses), handle timeout gracefully, and never make callouts in bulk trigger contexts without batching."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "12.3", number: 3, question: "Distinguishing fire-and-forget from request-reply and selecting fire-and-forget for long-running external processes where Salesforce does not need an immediate response", answer: "Fire-and-forget: Salesforce sends a message/event and immediately continues without waiting for a response. Use when: the process is long-running, Salesforce doesn't need the result, or the external system is unreliable. Implementation: Platform Events (published after DML commit), outbound messaging, Queueable with @future(callout=true) where response is ignored. This pattern improves Salesforce performance and resilience compared to waiting for external completion."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "12.4", number: 4, question: "Contrasting Remote Process Invocation (Request-Reply) with fire-and-forget when no callback is needed from the remote process", answer: "Request-Reply requires the caller to wait and process a response. Fire-and-forget does not. When the remote process produces a result that Salesforce doesn't need (e.g., sending a notification email via external service, triggering a workflow in another system), fire-and-forget is more efficient. It frees the Salesforce transaction faster, reduces coupling, and eliminates the risk of the external system's latency affecting Salesforce users. Use Request-Reply only when the response directly affects the Salesforce record or the current user interaction."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "12.5", number: 5, question: "Applying the batch data synchronization pattern when near-real-time is not required and operational impact must be minimized during off-hours", answer: "Batch sync: large volumes of data transferred at scheduled intervals (nightly, hourly). Use when: latency SLA is measured in hours, the data volume is too large for event-by-event processing, or the business process only requires periodic refresh (daily financial reports, weekly CRM enrichment). Schedule during low-traffic windows to minimize load on both systems. Implementation: Scheduled Apex + Bulk API, or ETL tool (Informatica, MuleSoft Batch) with SFTP/file-based transfer. Monitor batch completion and set up alerts for failures."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "12.6", number: 6, question: "Choosing the publish-subscribe (pub/sub) pattern using Platform Events when decoupling producers and consumers is required for scalability", answer: "Pub/sub: a producer publishes events to a channel (Platform Event) without knowing who is listening. Multiple consumers subscribe independently and process events at their own pace. Benefits: producers and consumers can scale, deploy, and fail independently. Adding a new consumer doesn't require changing the producer. In Salesforce, Platform Events are published from Apex/Flow and consumed via Flow triggers, Apex triggers, CometD clients, or MuleSoft connector."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "12.7", number: 7, question: "Choosing between Change Data Capture (CDC) and outbound messaging/workflow rules for event-driven outbound integration from Salesforce", answer: "CDC: automatically publishes change events (Create, Update, Delete, Undelete) for enabled objects. No code required to publish. Enriched payloads include changed header metadata. Supports replay. Best for external systems that need to stay in sync with Salesforce data changes. Outbound Messaging: workflow-based, sends XML SOAP messages when a rule fires. Simpler but limited to workflow criteria, no replay, requires endpoint acknowledgment. CDC is the modern recommended approach; outbound messaging is legacy."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "12.8", number: 8, question: "Selecting the data virtualization (External Objects) pattern to surface external data inside Salesforce without replication and understanding when it is preferred over batch synchronization", answer: "Data virtualization (Salesforce Connect): external data is queried in real time via OData or custom Apex adapter. No data stored in Salesforce. Preferred over batch sync when: data freshness is critical (always current), the external dataset is too large to replicate, the data is read-mostly, or replication would violate data residency. Not preferred when: complex SOQL joins across external and internal objects are needed, frequent write-back is required, or the external system has availability/latency issues."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "12.9", number: 9, question: "Applying the process choreography pattern when a business process spans multiple systems and no central orchestrator is appropriate", answer: "Choreography: each system reacts to events published by other systems. No central controller. Example: Order created in Salesforce > publishes OrderCreated event > Warehouse subscribes and picks items > publishes OrderShipped event > Salesforce subscribes and updates status. Appropriate when: systems are independently owned, loose coupling is critical, and the process can tolerate eventual consistency. Challenges: hard to visualize the full process flow and complex to debug distributed failures."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "12.10", number: 10, question: "Selecting the orchestration pattern using MuleSoft or a middleware layer when a single integration must coordinate multiple backend system calls", answer: "Orchestration: a central coordinator (MuleSoft flow, Apex service class) calls multiple systems in sequence or parallel, aggregates results, handles errors, and returns a unified response. Example: a quote creation in Salesforce triggers MuleSoft, which calls pricing service, inventory service, and tax service, then writes back to Salesforce. Appropriate when strict ordering is required, error handling must coordinate multiple systems, or complex transformation aggregates data from many sources."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "12.11", number: 11, question: "Identifying the UI Update or Remote Call-In pattern as appropriate when an external system needs to push updates into Salesforce without waiting for a Salesforce polling cycle", answer: "Remote Call-In: an external system proactively calls Salesforce APIs to push data (REST/SOAP, Apex REST, or Bulk API). Used when the external system is the event source and Salesforce is the target. Eliminates Salesforce polling and provides immediate updates. UI Update variant: the external system calls Salesforce, which triggers a Platform Event that updates a Lightning component in real time via the Streaming API. Design the inbound endpoint with OAuth, idempotency (external ID upsert), and proper HTTP status codes.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "pia_obj_13",
                    title: "Given a use case, define the components which create a solution that meets business requirements",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "13.1", number: 1, question: "Specifying the components of a Salesforce outbound integration using Apex callouts (HttpRequest, named credentials, remote site settings, async vs sync context)", answer: "Apex outbound callout components: HttpRequest (constructs the HTTP request with method, endpoint, headers, body), HttpResponse (receives the response), Http.send() (executes the callout). Named Credential: replaces hardcoded endpoint/credentials with a metadata reference (callout:MyNamedCred/path). Remote Site Settings: required when NOT using Named Credentials; allowlists the external domain. Async vs sync: in synchronous Apex, callouts are allowed unless DML preceded them. For post-DML callouts, use @future(callout=true) or Queueable."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "13.2", number: 2, question: "Selecting appropriate Salesforce integration components (External Services, Flow, Apex) for a low-code vs pro-code integration solution based on team skills and requirements", answer: "External Services: imports an OpenAPI spec and generates invocable actions usable in Flow. No code required. Best for admin-configured simple request-reply integrations. Flow: orchestrates External Services actions, transforms data with formulas, handles branching. Best for declarative automation triggered by records, screens, or schedules. Apex: full programmatic control, handles complex transformations, bulk processing, and error handling. Best for high-volume, complex, or performance-sensitive integrations. Choose based on team skills, complexity, and long-term maintainability."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "13.3", number: 3, question: "Identifying the components of an event-driven Salesforce integration using Platform Events (event producer, Platform Event definition, CometD subscriber, replay mechanism)", answer: "Platform Event definition: a custom object in Salesforce Setup defining the event's schema (fields). Event producer: Apex (EventBus.publish()), Flow, or Process Builder publishes the event after the transaction commits. CometD subscriber: an external system subscribes to /event/MyEvent__e via CometD protocol. Replay mechanism: each event has a ReplayId. Subscribers store the last processed ReplayId and reconnect with it to replay missed events (up to 3 days retention). Apex triggers on the event provide in-org processing."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "13.4", number: 4, question: "Defining the components of a MuleSoft-based integration (Anypoint Platform, API Gateway, Mule flows, connectors) and their roles in a Salesforce integration architecture", answer: "Anypoint Platform: the cloud management plane for designing, deploying, and monitoring Mule applications. API Gateway (API Manager): enforces security policies (OAuth, rate limiting, IP allowlisting) on API calls before they reach backend systems. Mule flows: define the integration logic (source > transformation > target). Connectors: pre-built adapters for Salesforce, databases, and SaaS apps (Salesforce Connector handles OAuth, SOQL, DML, and streaming). CloudHub: MuleSoft's managed cloud runtime for deploying Mule apps."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "13.5", number: 5, question: "Defining the components of a Salesforce Connect (OData) integration: External Data Source, External Object, OData adapter, and how they surface external records in Salesforce", answer: "External Data Source: configured in Salesforce Setup, points to the external OData endpoint URL with authentication details. OData adapter: the protocol handler built into Salesforce Connect that translates SOQL queries into OData requests. External Object: the Salesforce object (suffix __x) that represents the external data. Users interact with it like a standard object (List Views, detail pages, SOQL). External Lookup relationships link External Objects to standard objects. Each query triggers a real-time OData call to the external system."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "13.6", number: 6, question: "Defining the components of a Salesforce-to-Salesforce (SF2SF) integration: Connection objects, Subscription objects, published records, and the limitations of the native SF2SF feature", answer: "SF2SF components: Connection object (represents the relationship between two Salesforce orgs, established via a connection invitation); Subscription object (defines which object and fields the subscribing org wants to receive). Published records: the publishing org uses a Publish checkbox/button to share records. Limitations: only standard objects supported, no custom objects, no complex relationship traversal, basic field mapping, no transformation logic, no error handling beyond email notifications, and limited monitoring."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "13.7", number: 7, question: "Defining the components required for a Heroku Connect integration solution (Heroku app, Postgres database, Heroku Connect add-on, mappings) and their responsibilities", answer: "Heroku app: the web/mobile application that reads and writes data to Postgres. Postgres database: the relational database on Heroku storing replicated Salesforce data. Heroku Connect add-on: the middleware layer that polls Salesforce for changes and syncs them to Postgres (and vice versa). Mappings: configuration defining which Salesforce objects and fields to sync, polling intervals, and conflict resolution. The app never calls Salesforce APIs directly; all Salesforce data access goes through Postgres via Heroku Connect's replication.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "pia_obj_14",
                    title: "Given a use case, identify the trade-offs, limitations, and constraints that meet the proposed solution",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "14.1", number: 1, question: "Evaluating the trade-off between synchronous REST callouts (simplicity, immediate response) and asynchronous Platform Events (decoupling, scalability) for a given scenario", answer: "Synchronous REST callouts: simple to implement, immediate response, easy to debug. Trade-offs: caller blocked during callout, coupled to external system availability, 120-second timeout, not possible after DML. Platform Events: decoupled, scalable, producer/consumer can fail independently, supports replay and at-least-once delivery. Trade-offs: eventual consistency (response is not immediate), more complex to implement and monitor, debugging distributed event flows is harder. Choose sync when the result is needed immediately; choose async when decoupling, volume, or resilience matters more."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "14.2", number: 2, question: "Identifying the Salesforce callout timeout limit (120 seconds) and its impact on synchronous integration patterns involving long-running external processes", answer: "Apex callouts timeout at 120 seconds by default. If the external process takes longer, the callout throws a CalloutException. This hard limit makes synchronous callouts unsuitable for long-running external processes (report generation, large data processing). Alternatives: use the Continuation API for LWC/Aura to handle long-running callouts without blocking an Apex thread; use fire-and-forget (Platform Event) to trigger the external process and have the external system call back to Salesforce when complete."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "14.3", number: 3, question: "Understanding the 50,000 Platform Events per hour org limit and how it constrains high-volume event-driven integration designs", answer: "Salesforce limits published Platform Events to 50,000 per hour per org (25,000 for some editions). High-volume scenarios (IoT data, high-frequency order processing) can hit this limit. Mitigation strategies: batch multiple changes into a single event payload, use CDC which has a separate limit (250,000 events/day), use external event buses (Kafka, Anypoint MQ) for very high volumes and bridge to Salesforce for final record writes via Bulk API. Monitor usage in Setup > Platform Events and alert before limits are reached."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "14.4", number: 4, question: "Assessing the trade-offs between using Salesforce Connect (OData) for external data virtualization vs full data replication into Salesforce", answer: "Salesforce Connect (External Objects): pros: always current data, no storage cost, no sync complexity. Cons: every field access triggers a callout, no offline availability, limited SOQL (no aggregate functions, limited relationships), dependent on external system availability. Full data replication: pros: fast SOQL, full Salesforce feature support (reports, workflows, triggers), data available even if external system is down. Cons: sync lag, storage costs, transformation complexity, potential data conflicts. Use Connect for large read-only reference datasets; use replication when full Salesforce functionality is needed."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "14.5", number: 5, question: "Analyzing Heroku Connect replication lag and eventual consistency as trade-offs when near-real-time accuracy is required", answer: "Heroku Connect uses a polling-based sync model with a minimum polling interval of approximately 2 minutes, meaning changes in Salesforce take at least 2 minutes to appear in Postgres. This eventual consistency is acceptable for customer portals displaying account history but unacceptable for real-time inventory or live pricing. For near-real-time requirements, supplement with Platform Events: the Heroku app subscribes to CDC/Platform Events via a separate consumer to receive immediate notifications alongside the Heroku Connect base sync."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "14.6", number: 6, question: "Evaluating MuleSoft iPaaS cost and operational complexity trade-offs versus native Salesforce integration capabilities for simpler use cases", answer: "MuleSoft benefits: rich connector library, visual development, built-in monitoring, API management, rich error handling, and support for complex transformations. Costs: significant licensing fees, requires MuleSoft expertise to operate, adds operational overhead. Native Salesforce (Apex, Flow, External Services): lower cost, no additional platform, Salesforce admins/developers can maintain. Limitations: less sophisticated error handling, limited transformation capabilities, less visibility. Use native Salesforce for simple, low-volume, Salesforce-centric integrations. Use MuleSoft when integrating multiple non-Salesforce systems or needing enterprise-grade monitoring and governance."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "14.7", number: 7, question: "Evaluating the trade-offs of a native Salesforce-to-Salesforce (SF2SF) connection versus a middleware-brokered multi-org integration (data fidelity, error handling, SOQL relationship support)", answer: "Native SF2SF: zero additional cost, simple to configure. Trade-offs: limited to standard objects, no custom objects, no complex field mapping/transformation, no error handling beyond email, no monitoring, limited SOQL relationship support in the subscribing org. Middleware-brokered (MuleSoft): supports custom objects, bidirectional sync, complex transformation, rich error handling, full monitoring, SOQL relationship traversal. Choose native SF2SF only for simple standard-object sharing between trusted orgs with minimal transformation needs.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),
        // Salesforce Integration Technologies (22 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "salesforce_technologies",
            title: "Salesforce Integration Technologies",
            color: Color(red: 0.31, green: 0.27, blue: 0.90),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.93, green: 0.95, blue: 1.00),
            icon: "cloud.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "pia_obj_15",
                    title: "Given a use case that includes technical requirements, constraints, or drivers, specify the appropriate Salesforce application programming interface(s) (API) for the proposed solution",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "15.1", number: 1, question: "Choosing the REST API vs SOAP API based on data format requirements, client capability, and integration complexity", answer: "REST API: JSON or XML, stateless, lightweight, modern. Use when the external system supports REST, you need flexibility in payload format, or you're building web/mobile integrations. Salesforce REST API supports more modern features (Composite, GraphQL). SOAP API: XML only, WSDL-defined contract, strict typing. Use when the external system is legacy and only supports SOAP, or when the enterprise WSDL's strongly-typed object model simplifies client generation. REST is the default choice; SOAP when forced by the external system."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "15.2", number: 2, question: "Selecting the Bulk API 2.0 for high-volume data loads (greater than 2,000 records) and understanding its job-based asynchronous processing model", answer: "Bulk API 2.0 is optimized for large data volumes using a job-based model: create a job, upload records as CSV, close the job, poll for completion, and retrieve success/error results. Advantages over REST for bulk: processes millions of records efficiently, optimized for Salesforce's distributed infrastructure, separate API limit treatment. Key details: CSV format only, async processing, supports insert, update, upsert, delete, and hardDelete. Use when volumes exceed what REST Collections handle efficiently (>2,000 records per operation)."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "15.3", number: 3, question: "Applying the Composite API (composite resources, SObject Collections, composite graph) to reduce API call consumption for multi-record operations", answer: "Composite API resources: Composite (up to 25 subrequests in one HTTP call, can reference responses between subrequests using reference IDs), SObject Collections (up to 200 records in one insert/update/delete/upsert call with allOrNone or partial success), Composite Graph (create a complex graph of up to 500 nodes with relationships in a single call). Use to reduce API call consumption and improve atomicity for related operations. Example: create an Account, then use its ID to create a Contact and Opportunity in the same request."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "15.4", number: 4, question: "Selecting Platform Events when custom event payloads and app-defined events are required for streaming", answer: "Platform Events are custom Salesforce objects that define an event schema (any fields you need). Published from Apex, Flow, or external REST calls. Subscribed to via CometD (/event/EventName__e), Apex triggers, Flow triggers, or MuleSoft. Use when: you need custom event payloads beyond what CDC provides, you want to trigger Salesforce automation from an external system via a published event, or you need to publish events with custom data to external subscribers. Platform Events also provide replay capability via ReplayId."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "15.5", number: 5, question: "Selecting Change Data Capture when external systems must subscribe to Salesforce record changes via the Streaming API", answer: "CDC automatically publishes change events for enabled Salesforce objects (standard and custom) whenever records are created, updated, deleted, or undeleted. External systems subscribe via CometD (/data/ObjectNameChangeEvent) or MuleSoft CDC connector. CDC events include: changed field values, header metadata (changeType, changedFields, entityName), and commitUser. No code required to publish. Use when external systems need to replicate Salesforce data changes in near-real-time."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "15.6", number: 6, question: "Selecting PushTopic or Generic Streaming for SOQL-based subscription to record changes when custom event definitions are not needed", answer: "PushTopic: creates a subscription based on a SOQL query. Subscribers receive notifications when records matching the SOQL query are created, updated, deleted, or undeleted. Use when filtering by specific field criteria. Limited to 50,000 notifications per day. Legacy; Salesforce recommends CDC for most use cases now. Generic Streaming: allows any system to publish to a generic channel; Salesforce broadcasts to all subscribers. Use for custom non-record-based events where Platform Events would be overkill. Both use CometD."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "15.7", number: 7, question: "Choosing the Metadata API vs Tooling API for deployment and configuration automation use cases in CI/CD pipelines", answer: "Metadata API: retrieves and deploys metadata components (classes, triggers, layouts, custom objects, flows) as XML files. The standard for CI/CD pipelines (Salesforce CLI wraps it). Use for full deployment packages, sandbox-to-production promotion, and version control integration. Tooling API: provides fine-grained programmatic access to metadata at the component level, real-time compilation, and test execution. Better for IDE integrations (VS Code Salesforce Extension), just-in-time compile checks, and running specific tests. In CI/CD: Metadata API for deployments; Tooling API for IDE-level tooling and unit test execution."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "15.8", number: 8, question: "Applying the GraphQL API (Salesforce) for flexible data querying to reduce over-fetching in mobile or external portal integrations", answer: "Salesforce GraphQL API (GA in Spring '23) allows clients to specify exactly which fields they need in a single query, eliminating over-fetching (REST returns all fields) and under-fetching (REST requires multiple calls for related data). Ideal for mobile apps and external portals where bandwidth is limited and multiple related objects need to be queried efficiently. GraphQL queries can span parent-child relationships in one request. Use when REST requires multiple round-trips or returns more data than needed.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "pia_obj_16",
                    title: "Given a use case that includes technical requirements, constraints, or drivers, determine the standards, components, techniques, and security mechanism that should be used",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "16.1", number: 1, question: "Applying API-led connectivity (system layer, process layer, experience layer) using MuleSoft to design a reusable integration architecture", answer: "API-led connectivity defines three layers: System APIs (thin wrappers around source systems like Salesforce, SAP, databases, exposing raw capabilities), Process APIs (orchestrate system API calls, apply business logic, data enrichment, and transformation), Experience APIs (tailored to specific consumers, mobile app, web portal, partner, consuming process APIs). Each layer is independently deployable and reusable. Change one system API without affecting consumers; multiple experience APIs reuse the same process API."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "16.2", number: 2, question: "Applying the OpenAPI Specification (OAS/Swagger) or RAML to define and document integration APIs, and how API-first design improves integration reliability and reuse", answer: "API-first design: define the API contract (OAS 3.0/Swagger or RAML) before building. The contract describes endpoints, request/response schemas, authentication, and error codes. Benefits: parallel development (consumers code to the contract while the provider builds), auto-generated client SDKs, automated contract testing, and better documentation. In MuleSoft: design in Anypoint Design Center using RAML or OAS. Salesforce External Services can import an OAS spec to auto-generate invocable Flow actions."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "16.3", number: 3, question: "Applying data transformation standards (XSLT, DataWeave, JSON-to-JSON mapping) in integration middleware to translate between Salesforce's data model and external system schemas", answer: "XSLT: XML transformation language for converting XML documents (legacy SOAP systems). DataWeave (MuleSoft): powerful transformation language handling JSON, XML, CSV, Java objects, and more. Supports complex mapping, filtering, and aggregation. JSON-to-JSON mapping: simple key remapping using JSONata or custom code for lightweight scenarios. In Salesforce, Flow formulas and Apex JSON classes handle basic transformations. Choose DataWeave for complex multi-format transformations in MuleSoft; use Apex for Salesforce-native transformations."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "16.4", number: 4, question: "Applying OAuth 2.0 JWT Bearer flow for server-to-server integrations where no user interaction is possible and a service account is required", answer: "JWT Bearer token flow: the client constructs a JWT with claims (iss=client ID, sub=username, aud=Salesforce token endpoint, exp=expiry). Signs it with the private key of a certificate uploaded to the Connected App. Posts the JWT to /services/oauth2/token as an assertion. Salesforce validates the signature and returns an access token. No refresh token needed; just regenerate the JWT when the access token expires. Pre-authorize the user in the Connected App to skip the consent step. Ideal for CI/CD pipelines, scheduled jobs, and daemon processes."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "16.5", number: 5, question: "Selecting an API gateway (MuleSoft API Manager, AWS API Gateway) as the security enforcement point for inbound Salesforce integration requests and understanding its rate limiting and policy application role", answer: "An API gateway sits in front of Salesforce (or the integration layer) and enforces: authentication (OAuth token validation), authorization (role-based access), rate limiting (prevent a single consumer from exhausting API limits), IP allowlisting, TLS termination, and logging. Consumers call the gateway, not Salesforce directly. The gateway validates and forwards authorized requests. Benefits: centralized policy management, consumer analytics, throttling per consumer, and the ability to evolve backend systems without changing consumer contracts."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "16.6", number: 6, question: "Applying HMAC or digital signature verification for validating the authenticity and integrity of inbound webhook payloads to Salesforce", answer: "When an external system sends webhooks to Salesforce via an Apex REST endpoint, you must verify the request is authentic. HMAC: the sender computes an HMAC-SHA256 hash of the payload using a shared secret and includes it in the request header (e.g., X-Hub-Signature). The Salesforce endpoint recomputes the HMAC and compares it. Digital signatures: the sender signs the payload with its private key; Salesforce verifies using the sender's public certificate. Implement verification in the Apex @RestResource method before processing. Reject requests with invalid signatures."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "16.7", number: 7, question: "Selecting message queuing (e.g., MuleSoft VM queues, external MQ) as a buffering technique to handle traffic spikes between systems with different processing rates", answer: "Message queues decouple the producer (sending messages) from the consumer (processing messages). When the producer generates messages faster than the consumer can handle, the queue buffers the excess. The consumer processes at its own pace. Options: MuleSoft VM queues (in-memory, single Mule app), Anypoint MQ (persistent, multi-app, cloud), RabbitMQ, AWS SQS, or Azure Service Bus. For Salesforce integrations: queue incoming requests during maintenance windows, throttle outbound calls to respect external rate limits, and ensure at-least-once delivery for critical messages."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "16.8", number: 8, question: "Designing a Salesforce-connected MuleSoft Anypoint Platform deployment model (CloudHub, on-premise, hybrid) and selecting the appropriate runtime based on data residency and network requirements", answer: "CloudHub: fully managed, multi-tenant cloud runtime on AWS. Best for cloud-to-cloud integrations with no data residency restrictions. CloudHub 2.0 offers region selection. On-premise: Mule Runtime Engine deployed in your own data center. Required for accessing on-premise systems, strict data residency, or PCI/HIPAA environments. Hybrid: combination of on-premise runtime with cloud Anypoint Platform management. Provides local data processing with cloud monitoring. Choose based on where source/target systems are hosted, regulatory requirements, and internal network access constraints.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "pia_obj_17",
                    title: "Given a use case that includes technical requirements, constraints, or drivers, identify the considerations when designing and implementing an API, both Salesforce as an API provider and Salesforce as an API consumer",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "17.1", number: 1, question: "Designing Salesforce as an API provider: versioning strategy, resource URL design, authentication requirements, and rate limit exposure to consumers", answer: "Versioning: include the API version in the URL (e.g., /v1/orders) or header; increment major version on breaking changes. Resource URL design: use nouns (not verbs), follow REST conventions (/accounts/{id}/contacts). Authentication: require OAuth 2.0 access tokens; configure the Connected App with appropriate scopes. Rate limit exposure: Salesforce's API limits are shared; heavy consumers should use their own Connected App so usage is tracked separately. Use @RestResource for Apex-based custom APIs with @HttpGet, @HttpPost methods."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "17.2", number: 2, question: "Designing a custom REST API in Apex (@RestResource) as a Salesforce API provider and understanding its security, versioning, and governor limit considerations", answer: "Custom Apex REST: annotate the class with @RestResource(urlMapping='/myresource/*') and methods with @HttpGet, @HttpPost, etc. Security: governed by the authenticated user's profile and sharing rules (with sharing enforced). Versioning: embed version in urlMapping (e.g., /v1/myresource). Governor limits apply fully: SOQL, DML, and CPU limits all count against the transaction. Return RestResponse with appropriate HTTP status codes (200, 201, 400, 500). Test with HttpCalloutMock. Not suitable for very high throughput scenarios."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "17.3", number: 3, question: "Designing a Salesforce SOAP (WSDL-based) API provider and when to use the enterprise vs partner WSDL for external system consumption", answer: "Salesforce exposes two WSDLs: Enterprise WSDL: strongly typed, generated for a specific org's schema. Best for a single org with stable metadata. Partner WSDL: loosely typed, generic, works across any org and is version-stable. Best for ISV products, multi-org tools, or when the org schema changes frequently. Download WSDLs from Setup > API. External systems import the WSDL to generate client stubs. SOAP is less common for new integrations but required by legacy Java/.NET systems consuming WSDL-defined contracts."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "17.4", number: 4, question: "Implementing Salesforce as an API consumer using Apex HttpCallout: handling callout limits, timeout configuration, and callout-from-trigger restrictions", answer: "Apex HTTP callout: use Http.send(HttpRequest) to call external APIs. Set timeout: request.setTimeout(30000) (30 seconds, max 120,000ms). Handle callout limits: max 100 per transaction, cannot be called after DML. Callout-from-trigger restriction: triggers cannot make synchronous callouts if DML has already executed. Workarounds: @future(callout=true) for simple async, Queueable implements Queueable, Database.AllowsCallouts for chainable async. Always check getStatusCode() and branch on 2xx vs 4xx vs 5xx responses."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "17.5", number: 5, question: "Understanding the restriction that Apex callouts cannot be made after DML in the same transaction and the design patterns to work around it (Queueable, Platform Events)", answer: "Salesforce prevents callouts after DML in the same transaction to avoid partial-commit data corruption. Workaround patterns: @future(callout=true): runs after the transaction commits, simple but no chaining. Queueable with Database.AllowsCallouts: runs after commit, can chain, can pass complex objects. Platform Event: publish an event from the trigger (committed atomically with the triggering DML); a separate Apex trigger on the event processes the callout in its own clean transaction. Platform Events is the most robust and scalable pattern."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "17.6", number: 6, question: "Configuring External Services in Salesforce to register an external REST API and invoke it declaratively via Flow or Apex", answer: "External Services: upload or paste an OpenAPI 2.0 spec into Setup > External Services. Salesforce parses the spec and generates invocable actions for each endpoint. These actions appear in Flow as Action elements and can be called from Apex via the invocable method framework. Named Credentials are used for authentication. Limitations: only supports OpenAPI 2.0 (not 3.0 at most versions), limited complex schema support. Best for admin-managed, low-code integration scenarios with simple CRUD-like external APIs.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),
        // Middleware, External APIs & Error Handling (23 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "middleware_patterns",
            title: "Middleware, External APIs & Error Handling",
            color: Color(red: 0.83, green: 0.18, blue: 0.18),
            lightColor: Color(red: 1.00, green: 0.93, blue: 0.93),
            icon: "server.rack",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "pia_obj_18",
                    title: "Given a use case, identify the considerations when choosing the right option in making an outbound call to an external system",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "18.1", number: 1, question: "Selecting Named Credentials for secure credential management in outbound callouts and understanding per-user vs org-wide named credential configurations", answer: "Named Credentials store endpoint URL and authentication (OAuth, Basic, Custom headers) securely in Salesforce metadata. Reference in Apex as 'callout:MyNamedCred'. Two authentication types: Org-wide (all callouts use the same credentials, suitable for service accounts) and Per-user (each user's callout uses their own OAuth token, enforcing user-level access at the external system). Per-user requires an Auth Provider linked to the Named Credential. Named Credentials also handle token refresh automatically."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "18.2", number: 2, question: "Choosing between synchronous Apex callouts, Queueable Apex with callout, and Platform Events for outbound integration based on transaction context and volume", answer: "Synchronous Apex callout: inline in the current transaction. Use when the result is needed immediately (UI, decision logic) and no DML has occurred. Queueable Apex with callout: runs after the triggering transaction, can call external systems, chainable for sequential processing. Use for post-commit outbound calls that don't need an immediate response. Platform Events: publish an event; an external system receives and processes via CometD or MuleSoft. Use for high-volume fan-out, decoupled delivery, or when multiple consumers need the same notification."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "18.3", number: 3, question: "Evaluating Salesforce outbound messaging (workflow-based) vs Apex callouts vs Platform Events for pushing Salesforce data changes to external systems", answer: "Outbound Messaging: declarative, SOAP-based, sends XML to a listener when a workflow rule fires. Built-in retry up to 24 hours. No code. Limitations: SOAP only, limited payload customization. Apex callouts: flexible, REST or SOAP, complex transformation possible, but requires coding and careful async handling. Platform Events: decoupled, supports multiple consumers, replay, and near-real-time delivery. Modern choice for scalable outbound integration. Choose outbound messaging for simple legacy SOAP integrations; Platform Events for modern scalable solutions."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "18.4", number: 4, question: "Implementing Salesforce Flow (Screen Flow, Autolaunched Flow, Scheduled Flow) as a declarative outbound integration mechanism via External Services or Apex actions", answer: "Flow types for integration: Autolaunched Flow: triggered by record changes, runs server-side, can call External Services actions or Apex invocable methods. Scheduled Flow: runs on a schedule, processes a collection of records, calls External Services for each. Screen Flow: user-initiated, can make external calls during user interaction and show results in the screen. Use External Services actions for simple REST APIs defined by OpenAPI spec. For complex logic, wrap in an Apex @InvocableMethod and call from Flow. Flows share the same callout and DML limits as Apex."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "18.5", number: 5, question: "Understanding how Change Data Capture (CDC) enables external systems to subscribe to Salesforce record changes without polling, and when to prefer CDC over outbound messaging", answer: "CDC pushes change events to subscribers the moment a Salesforce record is committed. External systems subscribe to CometD channels and receive enriched event payloads including changed fields and operation type. No polling required; near-real-time delivery. Prefer CDC over outbound messaging when: the external system needs to know which specific fields changed (CDC provides changedFields); you need replay capability; you need to track delete and undelete operations; or you want a modern, scalable, code-free publish mechanism."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "18.6", number: 6, question: "Configuring Change Data Capture: selecting entities to track, understanding enriched payloads (changedFields, header metadata), and implementing header-based change filtering in subscribers", answer: "Configure CDC: Setup > Change Data Capture > select entities to track (all custom objects plus supported standard objects like Account, Contact, Opportunity). Event payload structure: ChangeEventHeader (entityName, changeType, changedFields, commitUser, commitTimestamp, recordIds, sequenceNumber) followed by changed field values. changedFields: a list of field names actually modified (enables subscribers to ignore irrelevant changes). Header-based filtering: subscribers can inspect ChangeEventHeader before processing the body, e.g., only process Create events or only when a specific field changed.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "pia_obj_19",
                    title: "Given a use case, describe what should be considered when building a scalable solution",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "19.1", number: 1, question: "Applying bulk-safe Apex trigger patterns to prevent governor limit violations when integration volumes increase unexpectedly", answer: "Bulk-safe trigger patterns: never put SOQL, DML, or callout logic inside a for loop iterating over Trigger.new. Collect all IDs/data from Trigger.new into collections first. Perform one SOQL query using IN operator on the collection. Process all records in memory. Perform DML once on the full list. For integration callouts from triggers, queue all records for async processing (Queueable) rather than one callout per record. Use a handler class pattern to keep trigger logic testable and separated from the trigger file."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "19.2", number: 2, question: "Designing an integration solution to handle Salesforce API daily limit growth by distributing API usage across connected apps or using Bulk API for high-volume operations", answer: "As data volumes grow, API consumption increases. Strategies: use separate Connected Apps per integration so each consumer's usage is tracked and managed independently; switch high-volume REST operations to Bulk API (fewer total API calls for the same volume of records); implement caching in middleware to avoid redundant reads; use streaming (CDC, Platform Events) to eliminate polling API calls; distribute integration workloads to off-peak hours. Monitor daily limit consumption via the /limits endpoint and set alerts at 70% and 90% thresholds."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "19.3", number: 3, question: "Partitioning high-volume batch integration jobs using Batchable Apex with appropriate scope sizes and chained Queueable patterns to stay within execution limits at scale", answer: "Batchable Apex: execute() method processes a 'scope' of records (default 200, configurable 1-2000). Each execute() runs in its own transaction with fresh governor limits. Set scope size based on complexity: simple operations can use 2000; complex transformations may need 50-100 to avoid CPU/heap limits. For integrations with callouts: each execute() can make callouts (up to 100 per execute()), but use one batched callout per execute() chunk. Chained Queueable: when batch cannot make callouts from finish(), chain into a Queueable to continue processing."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "19.4", number: 4, question: "Evaluating Platform Event delivery guarantees (at-least-once delivery) and designing idempotent consumers to handle duplicate events at scale", answer: "Platform Events guarantee at-least-once delivery, meaning a subscriber may receive the same event more than once (especially after reconnection or replay). Consumers must be idempotent: processing the same event multiple times must produce the same result as processing it once. Implementation: store the event's ReplayId or a unique event field in a custom object and check before processing; use upsert with an external ID field so duplicate record creation fails gracefully; use a Set to deduplicate event IDs in a single batch execution."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "19.5", number: 5, question: "Using the ReplayId mechanism in Platform Events / Streaming API to recover from subscriber failures and replay missed events", answer: "Every Platform Event and CDC event has a ReplayId (a monotonically increasing integer). Subscribers store the last successfully processed ReplayId. On reconnect, subscribe with that ReplayId to replay missed events (Salesforce retains events for 3 days). Special values: -1 = subscribe to only new events from now; -2 = replay from the earliest stored event. In CometD, pass the ReplayId in the subscription extension. In Apex trigger consumers, track ReplayId in a custom object. This mechanism provides resilience against subscriber downtime without data loss."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "19.6", number: 6, question: "Applying horizontal scaling principles in MuleSoft (multiple workers, clustering) to meet throughput requirements for high-volume Salesforce integrations", answer: "MuleSoft horizontal scaling: in CloudHub, increase the number of workers (each worker is an independent Mule runtime instance). Workers process in parallel, multiplying throughput. Use persistent queues (Anypoint MQ) to distribute work across workers without duplication. For Salesforce integrations: ensure idempotency (multiple workers may process the same event), use a shared state store (Redis, database) for coordination, and ensure the Salesforce API limits can handle the increased throughput from multiple parallel workers."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "19.7", number: 7, question: "Designing Salesforce Connect (External Objects) custom adapters using Apex for scalable access to external systems not covered by standard OData adapters", answer: "When the external system doesn't expose an OData endpoint, implement a custom Apex adapter using the DataSource.Provider and DataSource.Connection interfaces. The adapter translates Salesforce SOQL queries into the external system's native API calls (REST, SOAP, custom). Implement query(), upsertRows(), and deleteRows() methods. For scalability: implement server-side filtering and pagination in query() to avoid fetching all records and filtering in Apex. Cache frequently-accessed reference data if the external system has rate limits."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "19.8", number: 8, question: "Designing Heroku Connect mappings with appropriate polling intervals and field selection to balance synchronization frequency with Salesforce API consumption", answer: "Heroku Connect mappings control which objects, fields, and direction to sync. Best practices: only map fields needed by the Heroku app (not all Salesforce fields) to reduce payload size and API consumption. Set polling intervals based on data freshness requirements: shorter intervals = more API calls = faster sync. Use write-back mappings only for fields the Heroku app modifies, not read-only reference data. Monitor Salesforce API consumption from Heroku Connect. Use Heroku Connect's accelerated sync (triggered by webhooks or CDC) to reduce polling frequency while maintaining freshness.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "pia_obj_20",
                    title: "Given a use case, determine error handling for different integration options",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "20.1", number: 1, question: "Distinguishing system errors (network failure, timeout) from application errors (validation failure, business rule violation) in integration error handling", answer: "System errors: infrastructure-level failures including network timeouts, DNS failures, HTTP 503 (service unavailable), connection refused, TLS handshake failures. These are transient and often resolve on retry. Application errors: logic-level failures including HTTP 400 (bad request), HTTP 422 (validation failure), business rule violations returned in response body. These usually cannot be resolved by retrying the same request without fixing the data. Retry system errors; route application errors to a human review queue."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "20.2", number: 2, question: "Applying appropriate error handling strategies for system errors (retry, timeout handling) versus application errors (validation feedback, business rule messaging) in integrations", answer: "System error strategies: retry with exponential backoff (wait 1s, 2s, 4s, 8s between attempts), set a max retry count (3-5), log each failure with full context. For timeouts: increase timeout if appropriate, or switch to async pattern. Application error strategies: parse the error response body to extract the specific validation message, log with the offending record's ID, route to a failed record queue (custom object or dead-letter queue), send a notification with actionable details so a human or automated job can fix and resubmit. Never silently discard application errors."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "20.3", number: 3, question: "Implementing retry logic with exponential backoff for transient failures in Apex callouts and external API integrations", answer: "Exponential backoff: after a failed attempt, wait an exponentially increasing interval before retrying (1s, 2s, 4s, 8s, capped at a max like 60s). Add jitter (randomness) to prevent thundering herd when many clients retry simultaneously. In Apex: since you cannot sleep in synchronous Apex, implement retry via chained Queueable jobs with a retry count stored on a custom object or passed as state. In MuleSoft: use the until-successful router with retryInterval and maxRetries. Log each attempt, limit total retries (e.g., 5), and route to dead-letter queue after exhaustion."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "20.4", number: 4, question: "Implementing idempotent upsert operations using Salesforce external IDs to safely reprocess failed records without creating duplicates", answer: "External ID: a custom field on a Salesforce object marked as External ID (and optionally Unique). Used in upsert operations to match records. Syntax: PATCH /services/data/vXX.X/sobjects/Account/MyExternalId__c/{externalIdValue}. If a record with that external ID exists, it updates; otherwise it inserts. This makes the operation idempotent: sending the same record multiple times due to retry results in one record, not duplicates. Define an external ID for every object involved in integration. Use in Bulk API upsert jobs with externalIdFieldName."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "20.5", number: 5, question: "Designing Salesforce outbound messaging acknowledgment handling and what happens when the external endpoint does not acknowledge receipt", answer: "Salesforce outbound messaging sends an XML SOAP message and expects an HTTP 200 with an Ack element in the SOAP body. If the endpoint returns a non-200 response or doesn't respond, Salesforce automatically retries with increasing intervals for up to 24 hours. After 24 hours without acknowledgment, the message is abandoned. The external endpoint must be idempotent since the same message may be delivered multiple times. Monitor unacknowledged messages in Setup > Outbound Message Delivery Failures."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "20.6", number: 6, question: "Handling partial success in Salesforce Bulk API jobs (allOrNone vs partial success) and determining appropriate error record handling strategies", answer: "Bulk API 2.0 uses partial success by default: successful records are committed, failed records are reported separately. Retrieve failed records via the /jobs/ingest/{jobId}/failedResults endpoint (returns a CSV with error details per record). allOrNone is not directly supported in Bulk API 2.0 (it's a REST/SOAP option). Strategy for failed records: log each failed record with its error reason to a custom object, send alerts for batches with high failure rates, and implement a resubmission job that retries only failed records after fixing the root cause using idempotent upsert."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "20.7", number: 7, question: "Designing error notification and alerting in MuleSoft flows using error handlers, on-error-continue vs on-error-propagate, and routing errors to a dedicated monitoring channel", answer: "MuleSoft error handling: on-error-continue: processes the error handler and continues the flow normally (useful for logging without failing the transaction). on-error-propagate: processes the error handler and re-throws the error to the calling flow (propagates failure upward). Error notification: in the error handler, use a Logger component to log structured error details, then route to a monitoring channel (email, Slack, PagerDuty connector). Dead-letter routing: on repeated failure, route the message payload to a persistent Anypoint MQ queue or database table for human review."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "20.8", number: 8, question: "Designing dead-letter queue patterns for failed Platform Event processing or MuleSoft flows to prevent data loss without blocking the integration pipeline", answer: "A dead-letter queue (DLQ) stores messages that cannot be processed after all retry attempts, preventing failed messages from blocking the main pipeline. For Platform Events: Apex trigger consumers cannot natively DLQ; store unprocessable events in a custom object (Integration_Error__c) with the event payload, error reason, and timestamp for manual reprocessing. For MuleSoft: configure Anypoint MQ with a dead-letter queue; after max redeliveries, messages are moved to the DLQ automatically. Set up monitoring alerts when the DLQ receives messages and implement a replay/reprocess job."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "20.9", number: 9, question: "Implementing compensating transactions or saga patterns in distributed integration scenarios where two-phase commit is not possible", answer: "In distributed systems, two-phase commit is rarely achievable. The saga pattern breaks a distributed transaction into a sequence of local transactions, each with a compensating transaction that undoes it if a later step fails. Example: Create Order in Salesforce > Reserve Inventory in ERP > Charge Payment. If payment fails, trigger compensating transactions: release inventory reservation and cancel the Salesforce order. Implement via choreography (each system publishes success/failure events) or orchestration (a coordinator calls compensating actions on failure). This achieves eventual consistency without distributed locks.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),
        // Security, Resilience & Monitoring (32 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "security_resilience",
            title: "Security, Resilience & Monitoring",
            color: Color(red: 0.40, green: 0.30, blue: 0.20),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.97, green: 0.95, blue: 0.93),
            icon: "shield.checkered",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "pia_obj_21",
                    title: "Given a use case, create a security solution for inbound or outbound integrations",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "21.1", number: 1, question: "Implementing OAuth 2.0 Web Server flow for inbound integrations where a third-party application accesses Salesforce data on behalf of a user", answer: "Web Server flow steps: (1) App redirects user's browser to Salesforce authorization endpoint with client_id, redirect_uri, response_type=code, and scope. (2) User logs in and consents. (3) Salesforce redirects to the app's redirect_uri with ?code=auth_code. (4) App server exchanges the code for access + refresh tokens via POST to /services/oauth2/token. (5) App uses the access token to call Salesforce APIs. Store refresh tokens securely. Configure the Connected App's callback URL to match the redirect_uri exactly."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "21.2", number: 2, question: "Applying Named Credentials with per-user authentication for outbound integrations that must maintain data access consistent with the running user's Salesforce permissions", answer: "Per-user Named Credentials: each Salesforce user authenticates to the external system using their own credentials (via an Auth Provider). The Named Credential stores individual tokens per user. When a Flow or Apex callout runs in a user's context, it uses that user's external system token. The external system can then enforce its own user-level access control. Configure: Create an Auth Provider (OAuth) for the external system, link it to the Named Credential as per-user auth. Users must authenticate once via the Auth Provider to establish their token."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "21.3", number: 3, question: "Configuring Connected App admin-approved users only policy to restrict integration access to pre-authorized users", answer: "Admin-approved users only policy: in Setup > Connected Apps > Manage > Edit Policies, set 'Permitted Users' to 'Admin approved users are pre-authorized'. Only users with profiles or permission sets explicitly granted access to this Connected App can authenticate via it. This prevents any Salesforce user from using the Connected App. Best practice for all integration-specific Connected Apps. Grant access by assigning the Connected App to the integration user's profile or via a permission set."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "21.4", number: 4, question: "Configuring Connected App OAuth policies: IP relaxation when network constraints require it for integration access", answer: "IP relaxation options: 'Enforce IP restrictions': respects the user's profile Login IP Range exactly (integration from outside those IPs fails). 'Relax IP restrictions with second factor': allows access from any IP but requires MFA. 'Relax IP restrictions': ignores IP restrictions entirely (highest risk). Use 'Enforce IP restrictions' when integration source IPs are known and 'Relax with second factor' for users who travel. Only use full relaxation as a last resort and compensate with other controls (JWT Bearer, mTLS, monitoring)."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "21.5", number: 5, question: "Configuring Connected App refresh token policy (expiration, revocation) for integration token management", answer: "Refresh token policy options: 'Refresh token is valid until revoked' (tokens never expire, simplest for server-to-server), 'Refresh token expires after N days/hours of non-use' (expiry based on inactivity), 'Immediately expire refresh token' (single use, most secure but requires re-auth each time). For server-to-server automation: use 'valid until revoked' and manage the token securely. Implement token revocation procedures: when an integration is decommissioned, revoke tokens immediately via Setup > Connected Apps > Manage Connected Apps."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "21.6", number: 6, question: "Configuring Salesforce as a SAML identity provider (IdP) for delegated authentication to external systems and understanding when Salesforce acts as the IdP vs service provider (SP)", answer: "Salesforce as IdP: users log into Salesforce and are automatically authenticated to external apps that trust Salesforce as an IdP. Configure in Setup > Identity Provider (generate/upload certificate, enable IdP). External apps are configured as Service Providers (SPs) receiving SAML assertions signed by Salesforce. Salesforce as SP: users log into their corporate IdP (Okta, Azure AD) and SSO into Salesforce (configure in Setup > Single Sign-On Settings). In Salesforce-centric organizations, Salesforce is more commonly the SP for employee SSO."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "21.7", number: 7, question: "Implementing certificate-based authentication (mutual TLS) for high-security outbound callouts and configuring the Salesforce certificate keystore", answer: "mTLS outbound from Salesforce: (1) Generate or import a client certificate in Setup > Certificate and Key Management. Salesforce supports self-signed and CA-signed certificates. (2) Export the certificate (public key) and provide it to the external system to trust. (3) In Apex callout: HttpRequest.setClientCertificateName('My_Certificate_Name'), or reference in Named Credential. When Salesforce connects, it presents the client certificate. The external system validates it against the trusted certificate. Renew certificates before expiry; treat private keys as highly sensitive."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "21.8", number: 8, question: "Configuring Salesforce Shield Platform Encryption for integration scenarios: impact on SOQL, Bulk API, and outbound data visibility when fields are encrypted", answer: "Shield Platform Encryption encrypts field values at the database level. Encrypted fields: cannot be used in SOQL WHERE clause filter conditions, cannot be sorted in SOQL, cannot be used in formula fields or roll-up summaries. For integrations: outbound REST API responses return encrypted fields as plaintext to authenticated users with 'View Encrypted Data' permission; Bulk API exports also return plaintext for authorized users. If the integration user lacks the permission, encrypted fields return masked values. Design integration queries to avoid WHERE filters on encrypted fields."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "21.9", number: 9, question: "Implementing the OAuth 2.0 Device Flow for IoT or headless integrations where a browser redirect is not possible", answer: "Device Flow: designed for devices without a browser (IoT sensors, CLI tools). Steps: (1) Device requests a device code and user code from Salesforce (/services/oauth2/token with grant_type=device). (2) Device displays the user code and verification URL. (3) User visits the URL on another device, enters the code, and authorizes. (4) Device polls Salesforce until authorization is granted and receives access + refresh tokens. Suitable for long-lived IoT integrations where human authorization is performed once during provisioning. Ensure the device stores resulting tokens securely.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "pia_obj_22",
                    title: "Given a use case, identify the factors needed to build resilience in an integration solution for system updates",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "22.1", number: 1, question: "Applying API versioning strategies in Salesforce integrations to prevent breaking changes when Salesforce deprecates older API versions", answer: "Salesforce maintains backward compatibility for API versions but eventually retires very old versions. Pin your Apex callouts and external connections to a specific Salesforce API version in the URL (e.g., /services/data/v59.0/). Test against the upcoming version in sandbox before each Salesforce release. For inbound custom Apex REST APIs: include the version in the URL mapping and maintain old versions alongside new ones until consumers migrate. Automate version compliance checks in CI/CD pipelines. Never use /services/data/latest/ in production."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "22.2", number: 2, question: "Using Custom Metadata Types or Custom Settings to externalize integration endpoint configuration, enabling environment-specific values to be deployed independently of Apex code", answer: "Custom Metadata Types (CMT): metadata records deployable via change sets/SFDX. Use for endpoint URLs, timeout values, feature flags, and integration configuration that differs per environment. CMTs are queryable in Apex without SOQL limits. Custom Settings: similar but less portable; hierarchical (org, profile, user levels). Store endpoint URLs in CMT so deploying the CMT record to production updates the endpoint without a code deployment. Pair with Named Credentials for credentials and CMT for endpoint paths."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "22.3", number: 3, question: "Designing a sandbox-to-production integration promotion strategy: managing Named Credentials, Connected App configurations, and endpoint URLs across environments", answer: "Integration environment promotion challenges: Named Credentials reference different endpoint URLs per environment (sandbox points to external sandbox, production points to external production). Connected Apps have environment-specific OAuth credentials. Strategy: use Custom Metadata Types to store environment-specific endpoint paths. Deploy Named Credentials via change sets but expect manual configuration of credentials in each environment (credentials are not deployed for security). Maintain a documented runbook for post-deployment integration configuration steps."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "22.4", number: 4, question: "Designing integrations resilient to Salesforce seasonal releases by avoiding hard dependencies on API version-specific behaviors and using stable API endpoints", answer: "Salesforce releases three major updates per year (Spring, Summer, Winter). Resilience strategies: pin API versions explicitly (don't use 'latest'); test integrations in sandbox during preview windows (4-6 weeks before release); avoid relying on undocumented behaviors or response field order; use official Salesforce APIs rather than internal endpoints; subscribe to Salesforce release notes for deprecation announcements. Automate integration regression tests that run during preview sandbox periods."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "22.5", number: 5, question: "Designing for Salesforce maintenance windows: understanding sandbox refresh impacts, scheduled maintenance, and how to build integrations that gracefully handle temporary unavailability", answer: "Salesforce scheduled maintenance: org-specific maintenance windows (usually weekends/nights) for instance upgrades. Sandbox refreshes: can take hours to days and leave the sandbox unavailable. Integration resilience: implement circuit breakers that detect Salesforce unavailability (HTTP 503 responses) and stop sending requests. Buffer incoming messages in a queue (Anypoint MQ) during downtime. Implement replay logic for streaming subscriptions (ReplayId). Document maintenance schedules and notify integration partners. Test graceful degradation in sandbox environments."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "22.6", number: 6, question: "Building integration test harnesses using Apex mock callouts (StaticResourceCalloutMock, HttpCalloutMock) to enable CI/CD pipeline testing without live external endpoints", answer: "Apex test classes cannot make live HTTP callouts; they must use mocks. HttpCalloutMock interface: implement respond(HttpRequest) to return a custom HttpResponse for your test scenario. Register with Test.setMock(HttpCalloutMock.class, myMock). StaticResourceCalloutMock: simpler; point to a static resource file containing the mock response body (good for large JSON payloads). Use MultiRequestMock to return different responses for different endpoints. Test happy path, timeout, validation errors, and system errors. Mock callouts enable full integration logic testing in CI/CD without external dependencies."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "22.7", number: 7, question: "Designing integration tests and monitoring that detect integration failures caused by Salesforce metadata changes (field removal, picklist changes, validation rules)", answer: "Salesforce metadata changes can silently break integrations: removing a field breaks outbound payloads that reference it; adding a required validation rule causes integration writes to fail; changing a picklist value causes incoming data to fail validation. Detection strategies: include field existence assertions in integration test suites; run integration smoke tests after every deployment; use Salesforce Describe API to validate field presence in middleware; monitor integration error rates and alert on spikes post-deployment. Treat metadata changes as integration change events requiring regression testing."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "22.8", number: 8, question: "Implementing circuit breaker patterns in integration middleware to prevent cascading failures when a downstream system (Salesforce or external) is unavailable", answer: "Circuit breaker pattern states: Closed (normal operation, all requests pass through); Open (failure threshold exceeded, all requests immediately fail without calling downstream, protecting the unavailable system from additional load); Half-Open (after a timeout, one test request is allowed through; success transitions to Closed, failure stays Open). In MuleSoft: implement with a stateful component tracking failure counts, or use Anypoint MQ as a buffer. Benefits: prevents thread exhaustion, allows faster recovery, and provides clear monitoring signals.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "pia_obj_23",
                    title: "Given an integration maintenance use case, identify performance monitoring needs for integration requirements",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "23.1", number: 1, question: "Defining KPIs and thresholds for integration monitoring (throughput, error rate, latency, queue depth) and mapping them to Salesforce API usage dashboards", answer: "Key integration KPIs: Throughput (messages/records processed per minute/hour), Error rate (percentage of failed transactions, alert at >1-2%), Latency (end-to-end processing time, alert when exceeding SLA threshold), Queue depth (number of messages waiting, alert when growing). Map to Salesforce: use custom Salesforce dashboards on Integration Log objects for throughput and error rate. Monitor API usage via Setup > System Overview or /limits endpoint. In MuleSoft: Anypoint Monitoring dashboards provide flow-level metrics. Set SLA-based alert thresholds and escalation paths for each KPI."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "23.2", number: 2, question: "Monitoring Salesforce API limit consumption using the /limits endpoint and setting up alerts before daily limits are exhausted", answer: "The Salesforce REST API /services/data/vXX.X/limits endpoint returns current usage and total for all governor limits including DailyApiRequests (REST/SOAP API calls), DailyBulkApiRequests, DailyStreamingApiEvents, etc. Poll this endpoint regularly from a monitoring tool and push metrics to a dashboard. Alert thresholds: warn at 70%, critical at 90% of daily limit. When limits approach exhaustion: identify the highest-consuming Connected App, switch high-volume operations to Bulk API, reduce polling frequency, or contact Salesforce to increase limits via a licensing upgrade."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "23.3", number: 3, question: "Using Salesforce Event Monitoring (EventLogFile) to audit API usage, identify integration performance bottlenecks, and detect unauthorized access patterns", answer: "Event Monitoring (requires add-on license) logs detailed API activity in EventLogFile objects. Log types relevant to integrations: ApiTotalUsage (all API calls with user, endpoint, response time), BulkApi events, and LightningApiEvent. Query via SOQL or download CSV files. Use to: identify which integration users/connected apps consume the most API calls, detect unusually high volumes suggesting loops/inefficiencies, audit off-hours API access (potential unauthorized access), and measure response time percentiles. Visualize in Salesforce Reports, Tableau, or external SIEM tools."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "23.4", number: 4, question: "Defining MuleSoft Anypoint Monitoring dashboards and alerts to track flow execution times, error rates, and message volumes for Salesforce integrations", answer: "Anypoint Monitoring provides: built-in dashboards for flow execution time, error counts, throughput, and JVM metrics. Custom dashboards: create charts tracking specific Salesforce-related flows (e.g., 'Account Sync Flow'). Alerts: configure threshold-based alerts (email, Slack, PagerDuty) when error rate exceeds X% or latency exceeds Y seconds. Log points: add structured logging in Mule flows with correlation IDs for end-to-end tracing. Use Anypoint Monitoring alongside Salesforce Event Monitoring for full end-to-end observability."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "23.5", number: 5, question: "Implementing Platform Event subscriber health checks (delivery status, replay position) to detect and recover from stalled streaming consumers", answer: "Platform Event subscriber health: monitor that subscribers are actively processing events and keeping up with the stream. Signals of a stalled subscriber: the subscriber's last processed ReplayId falls significantly behind the latest published ReplayId (events accumulating unprocessed). Detection: store the last processed ReplayId in a custom object and compare with the latest; set up a monitoring job that alerts if the gap exceeds a threshold. Recovery: the subscriber reconnects using the stored ReplayId to replay missed events (up to 3 days). Proactively monitor consumer uptime and reconnection logic."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "23.6", number: 6, question: "Using Salesforce transaction security policies and real-time event monitoring to detect anomalous integration access patterns (unusual API volumes, off-hours access)", answer: "Transaction Security Policies (TSP): declarative rules in Salesforce that evaluate real-time events (ApiEvent, LoginEvent, etc.) and trigger actions (block the operation, send a notification, require MFA) when conditions are met. Example: alert when a single user makes more than 1,000 API calls per hour, or when API calls originate from an unusual geographic location. Configure in Setup > Transaction Security. Use alongside Event Monitoring to detect anomalous access patterns that may indicate credential compromise or runaway integration loops.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "pia_obj_24",
                    title: "Given a use case, identify the appropriate error handling, escalation, and recovery procedures for a failed integration",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "24.1", number: 1, question: "Defining escalation paths for integration failures: when to auto-retry, when to alert operations, and when to trigger manual intervention based on error classification", answer: "Escalation path framework: Tier 1 (Auto-retry): transient system errors (network timeout, HTTP 503) are automatically retried with exponential backoff up to a retry limit. Tier 2 (Ops Alert): after retry exhaustion or for persistent failures, send automated alerts to the operations team via email, Slack, or PagerDuty. Tier 3 (Manual Intervention): application errors (validation failures, business rule violations) or failures requiring data correction are routed to a failed record queue and assigned to a human for investigation and resubmission."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "24.2", number: 2, question: "Designing outbound message retry behavior and understanding Salesforce's built-in retry for unacknowledged outbound messages (retries up to 24 hours)", answer: "Salesforce outbound messaging has built-in retry: if the external endpoint does not return an HTTP 200 with the SOAP Ack body, Salesforce retries with exponentially increasing intervals for up to 24 hours. After 24 hours without acknowledgment, the message is abandoned and a failure email is sent to the org's admin. This means external endpoints must be idempotent (the same message may be delivered multiple times). Monitor delivery failures in Setup > Outbound Message Delivery Failures. Supplement with custom logging in Apex triggers if you need detailed retry tracking."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "24.3", number: 3, question: "Implementing Platform Event replay (using stored ReplayId or -2 for earliest) for recovery when a subscriber was offline and missed events", answer: "When a Platform Event or CDC subscriber was offline and missed events, it can recover by replaying using the ReplayId stored before going offline. Subscribe with the last known ReplayId to receive all events published after that point (up to 3 days of retention). Use -2 to replay from the earliest available event (maximum catch-up). Use -1 to subscribe from now (no catch-up). Implement: store the last processed ReplayId in a persistent custom object or external database after each successful message processing. On reconnect, retrieve the stored ReplayId and pass it in the CometD subscription request."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "24.4", number: 4, question: "Designing recovery procedures for failed Bulk API jobs: partial success handling, error record reprocessing, and idempotent upsert strategies", answer: "Bulk API 2.0 recovery: after a job completes, retrieve failed records via /jobs/ingest/{jobId}/failedResults (CSV with errorCode and errorMessage per record). Log each failed record to a custom object (Bulk_Job_Error__c) with the record's external ID, error reason, and timestamp. Automated reprocessing: after human or automated root cause analysis, create a new Bulk API job with only the failed records (identified by external ID). Use upsert with external ID to ensure idempotency: records that were partially successful in the first run are safely updated rather than duplicated in the retry job.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "pia_obj_25",
                    title: "Given a use case, identify reporting needs for integration monitoring",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "25.1", number: 1, question: "Identifying Salesforce custom objects or platform tools (e.g., EventLogFile, custom logs) to capture integration execution history for reporting and troubleshooting", answer: "Options for integration logging in Salesforce: Custom Integration Log Object (e.g., Integration_Log__c) with fields for timestamp, source system, target system, operation type, record ID, status (Success/Error), error message, and payload (up to 131,072 characters in a Long Text field). EventLogFile (Event Monitoring add-on): captures API-level events automatically but requires querying and downloading CSV files. Platform Events: can be used to stream log events to an external logging system (Splunk, Datadog) in real time. Custom objects provide the most query-able and reportable option for business stakeholders."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "25.2", number: 2, question: "Using Salesforce Reports and Dashboards on custom integration log objects to give business stakeholders self-service visibility into integration health without requiring direct system access", answer: "Custom integration log objects (Integration_Log__c) support standard Salesforce Reports and Dashboards. Build reports showing: total integrations by status (Success/Error), error rates by source system, and trend lines over time. Dashboard components: gauge for current error rate vs SLA threshold, bar chart for volume by system, and a list of recent failures requiring attention. Share dashboards with business stakeholders via Salesforce access (no dev tools required). Schedule report emails for daily/weekly health summaries. Use Report Types to join log records to related Salesforce objects for context."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "25.3", number: 3, question: "Designing integration audit trail reports that capture message volumes, success/failure rates, and SLA compliance over time for stakeholder reporting", answer: "Audit trail report design: log each integration execution in a custom object with timestamps (start time, end time), volume processed, success count, failure count, and SLA target. Create Salesforce Report Types joining the log object to related records. Build matrix reports showing volume and error rates by day/week/month. SLA compliance: calculate processing time against the SLA threshold (e.g., percentage of records processed within 15 minutes). Schedule reports to run automatically and email to stakeholders. Archive older log records to Big Objects if long-term retention is required."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "25.4", number: 4, question: "Defining reporting requirements that distinguish transient failures (auto-recovered) from persistent failures (requiring business escalation) in integration monitoring dashboards", answer: "Transient failures: initially failed but successfully retried automatically. These should be logged with status 'Auto-Recovered' and tracked in a separate report metric (retry rate). Persistent failures: failed after all retry attempts and require human intervention. Log with status 'Failed - Pending Review' and trigger an automated alert. Dashboards should show: two separate gauges (transient vs persistent failure rates), a list of persistent failures pending review, and a trend line showing if the retry rate is increasing (early warning of a systemic issue). Distinguish these clearly so business stakeholders don't confuse auto-recovered events with true failures."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "25.5", number: 5, question: "Establishing integration governance reporting: tracking API consumer usage by connected app, enforcing rate limits per consumer, and providing capacity planning data to integration architects", answer: "Integration governance reporting: use the /limits REST endpoint and EventLogFile to extract API call counts per Connected App. Build a Salesforce Dashboard or external BI report showing: API consumption per Connected App as a percentage of daily limits, trends over 30/60/90 days for capacity planning, and Connected Apps approaching their fair-share threshold. Enforce rate limits: configure API gateway (MuleSoft API Manager) policies to throttle per Connected App. Provide monthly capacity planning reports to integration architects showing growth rates and projected limit exhaustion dates to inform licensing and architecture decisions.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        )
    ]
}
